LAB.pages = [
 {"id": "L2-obs-climate", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "weather", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide009_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Steep granite peaks with snow and glaciers above a lake, southern Andes", "cap": "A · southern Andes"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide009_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Bare, eroded, pale badland ridges with no vegetation, Atacama Desert", "cap": "B · Atacama Desert"}], "img": "img/L2/slide009_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · two climates", "q": "Which landscape has more chemical weathering? In the other landscape, what process does most of the weathering?", "seen": "<ul><li><b>A:</b> glaciers, snow, rain, a lake and some plants, on very steep mountains that are still rising.</li><li><b>B:</b> no plants, no water, sharp ridges shaped by wind, pale bare rock.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">A has much more chemical weathering, because it has water. It also has strong physical weathering from glaciers, gravity and steep slopes. B has almost only physical weathering, from wind blowing sand. Some parts of the Atacama have had almost no rain for millions of years. This is why minerals that normally dissolve in rain, such as nitrates, are still stable there. The other extreme is Antarctica. Everything is frozen and there is no liquid water, so there is almost no chemical weathering.</p>", "e": "A has much more chemical weathering, because it has water. It also has strong physical weathering from glaciers, gravity and steep slopes. B has almost only physical weathering, from wind blowing sand. Some parts of the Atacama have had almost no rain for millions of years. This is why minerals that normally dissolve in rain, such as nitrates, are still stable there. The other extreme is Antarctica. Everything is frozen and there is no liquid water, so there is almost no chemical weathering."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-granite", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "profiles", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide016_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A geologist beside a quarry face of pale, heavily jointed granite with orange-red iron staining along the joints"}], "img": "img/L2/slide016_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · Manuka open cut, south of Cobar", "q": "This was once a hard granite. List three things in the photo that show it is deeply weathered.", "seen": "<ul><li>Many joints and fractures cut the whole face. Water flowed along them.</li><li>The rock is pale (bleached) overall: iron has been removed from the rock.</li><li>Red-orange hematite stains are concentrated along the fractures, where that iron was deposited again.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">In the close-up (slide 17), only small pieces of fresh granite are left. The rest is quartz and clay, soft enough to dig. Weathering moves inwards from every fracture, so the last fresh rock is always in the centre of the blocks.</p>", "e": "In the close-up (slide 17), only small pieces of fresh granite are left. The rest is quartz and clay, soft enough to dig. Weathering moves inwards from every fracture, so the last fresh rock is always in the centre of the blocks."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-profile", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "profiles", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide019_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Open pit wall showing a reddish-brown upper layer, a pale bleached band, and darker iron-stained rock below, with water ponded in the pit floor"}], "img": "img/L2/slide019_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · Boundary South pit, Manuka mine", "q": "Describe this pit wall from top to bottom. What are the layers, and what did water do to make them?", "seen": "<ul><li><b>Top:</b> red-brown soil and weathered rock, rich in iron.</li><li><b>Middle:</b> a pale, bleached zone. You can still see bedding, but the iron has gone.</li><li><b>Bottom:</b> brown, iron-altered limestone, and then fresh rock.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">The water table moved up and down through this profile many times. Each time, it carried dissolved iron. It removed iron from the bleached zone and deposited it above and below. Iron is the element that groundwater moves in the most visible way. Also notice that the iron is not spread evenly: a fault through the pit controls where the high-iron bands are.</p>", "e": "The water table moved up and down through this profile many times. Each time, it carried dissolved iron. It removed iron from the bleached zone and deposited it above and below. Iron is the element that groundwater moves in the most visible way. Also notice that the iron is not spread evenly: a fault through the pit controls where the high-iron bands are."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-core18", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "profiles", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide020_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Drill core that is crumbly, pale orange and white, with no visible rock texture"}], "img": "img/L2/slide020_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · diamond core at 18 m, Copper Deeps, Drake Goldfield", "q": "This core is from 18 m down a drill hole. What rock was it?", "seen": "<p>You cannot tell. No structure is left at all, only kaolinite and hematite. That is the point of this task. In Western Australia you can drill through more than 100 m of strongly weathered material before you reach anything that looks like rock, because groundwater has been weathering the profile for millions of years. To find out what the original rock was, you use the chemistry of immobile elements (explained later in this lecture).</p>", "e": "You cannot tell. No structure is left at all, only kaolinite and hematite. That is the point of this task. In Western Australia you can drill through more than 100 m of strongly weathered material before you reach anything that looks like rock, because groundwater has been weathering the profile for millions of years. To find out what the original rock was, you use the chemistry of immobile elements (explained later in this lecture)."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-gossanAu", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "gossans", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide028_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Geologist standing in a shallow pit dug into red-brown gossan among scrubby trees"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide028_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Close-up of hard, dark red-brown gossan blocks next to a geological hammer"}], "img": "img/L2/slide028_2.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · an unknown gossan, southern Cobar field", "q": "No prospector or miner ever recorded this outcrop. Later, geologists found visible gold all through it, but no sulphide was left at all. Why did the gold survive when everything else oxidised?", "seen": "<p>Gold does not oxidise. The pyrite or pyrrhotite that once contained the gold changed completely to goethite. But the gold grains stayed. They even became relatively richer, because the rock around them lost mass. Two lessons: there are still undiscovered gossans, even in well-explored districts like Cobar. And you must assay a gossan sample for metals; you cannot judge it by how it looks.</p>", "e": "Gold does not oxidise. The pyrite or pyrrhotite that once contained the gold changed completely to goethite. But the gold grains stayed. They even became relatively richer, because the rock around them lost mass. Two lessons: there are still undiscovered gossans, even in well-explored districts like Cobar. And you must assay a gossan sample for metals; you cannot judge it by how it looks."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-tsumeb", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "deep", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide035_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Deep blue azurite crystals with pale green malachite", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide036_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Emerald-green crystals on white matrix", "cap": "B"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide036_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pink rhombohedral crystals in a cluster", "cap": "C"}], "img": "img/L2/slide035_2.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · Tsumeb specimens", "q": "All three are secondary minerals from the oxide zone. Which metal does each one contain? Why is C pink?", "seen": "<ul><li><b>A:</b> azurite (deep blue) with malachite (green). Both are copper hydroxyl carbonates.</li><li><b>B:</b> dioptase, a copper silicate with a very bright green colour. The dioptase from Tsumeb is among the best in the world.</li><li><b>C:</b> smithsonite, a zinc carbonate. Zinc has no colour of its own; the pink colour comes from a small amount of cobalt.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">In an oxide zone, bright colours (blue, green) usually mean copper. Colourless or white minerals are usually lead or zinc carbonates and sulphates (cerussite, smithsonite, anglesite), unless a trace element colours them.</p>", "e": "In an oxide zone, bright colours (blue, green) usually mean copper. Colourless or white minerals are usually lead or zinc carbonates and sulphates (cerussite, smithsonite, anglesite), unless a trace element colours them."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-kelly", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "deep", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide039_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Open pit wall with labelled bands: acid-leached zone, goethite halo, and enriched band"}], "img": "img/L2/slide039_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · Mount Kelly, north of Mount Isa", "q": "At Mount Kelly, only the oxide zone was mined. Which labelled band was the ore? How do you think it was processed?", "seen": "<p>The ore was the darker <b>enriched band</b> on the right. It is rich in hematite and copper oxides and carbonates (malachite, azurite). The acid-leached zone has lost its metal. The goethite halo shows where groundwater moved iron around it. The ore was crushed, piled on a pad and sprayed with acid for a few months. Then the solution with the dissolved copper was pumped away. This is called heap leaching, and it is one of the simplest metallurgical methods. The deposit is where the Mount Kelly Fault meets the regional McNamara Fault. The mineralised band is about 500 m long along strike.</p>", "e": "The ore was the darker enriched band on the right. It is rich in hematite and copper oxides and carbonates (malachite, azurite). The acid-leached zone has lost its metal. The goethite halo shows where groundwater moved iron around it. The ore was crushed, piled on a pad and sprayed with acid for a few months. Then the solution with the dissolved copper was pumped away. This is called heap leaching, and it is one of the simplest metallurgical methods. The deposit is where the Mount Kelly Fault meets the regional McNamara Fault. The mineralised band is about 500 m long along strike."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-altered", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "alter", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide042_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale, soft-looking drill core with a chalky texture", "cap": "A · altered volcanic tuff, core"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide042_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Thin section under the microscope with bright high-coloured grains", "cap": "B · thin section, Woodlark Island, PNG"}], "img": "img/L2/slide042_2.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · two altered rocks", "q": "In A, what happened to the feldspars? In B, the bright mineral with high interference colours is a common alteration mineral. Which mineral is it? Did it form at a higher or lower temperature than the alteration in A?", "seen": "<p>A: all the feldspars have changed to clay, because acidic hydrothermal fluid attacked them. The core is pale, chalky and soft. B: the bright mineral is epidote. Epidote forms at higher temperatures than clay (argillic) alteration. So these two rocks show two very different fluid temperatures, and you can tell this from the minerals alone.</p>", "e": "A: all the feldspars have changed to clay, because acidic hydrothermal fluid attacked them. The core is pale, chalky and soft. B: the bright mineral is epidote. Epidote forms at higher temperatures than clay (argillic) alteration. So these two rocks show two very different fluid temperatures, and you can tell this from the minerals alone."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-propphyl", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "assemblages", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide046_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Drill core of green rock with scattered pale and dark fragments", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide047_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Close-up of a pale grey-white rock with orange iron spots", "cap": "B"}], "img": "img/L2/slide046_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · green rock and pale rock", "q": "Name the alteration in each rock. Use only the colour and the minerals you would expect to find.", "seen": "<p>A is <b>propylitic</b>. The green colour comes from chlorite and epidote, and you would expect some scattered pyrite. B is <b>phyllic</b>. It is pale because the feldspar and ferromagnesian minerals have changed to fine white mica and quartz. The orange spots are where pyrite has weathered to iron oxide. A simple rule: green rock means propylitic, so look for pyrite.</p>", "e": "A is propylitic. The green colour comes from chlorite and epidote, and you would expect some scattered pyrite. B is phyllic. It is pale because the feldspar and ferromagnesian minerals have changed to fine white mica and quartz. The orange spots are where pyrite has weathered to iron oxide. A simple rule: green rock means propylitic, so look for pyrite."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-jaguar", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "zoning", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide063_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground face of dark green basalt pillows with paler bleached margins and sulphide along the pillow rims"}], "img": "img/L2/slide063_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · Jaguar VMS deposit, Western Australia", "q": "These are Archaean pillow basalts, more than 3 billion years old. Where did the sulphide go first, and why there?", "seen": "<p>The sulphide first replaced the edges between the pillows and filled joint cracks, because these are weak planes that fluid can enter. Strong chlorite alteration formed around the pillow edges, where fluid flow was weaker. Closer to the vent, the fluid flow was strong enough to bleach the basalt and replace whole pillows with chalcopyrite and pyrite. This is a replacement deposit: you can see the sulphide replacing the rock from the outside inwards.</p>", "e": "The sulphide first replaced the edges between the pillows and filled joint cracks, because these are weak planes that fluid can enter. Strong chlorite alteration formed around the pillow edges, where fluid flow was weaker. Closer to the vent, the fluid flow was strong enough to bleach the basalt and replace whole pillows with chalcopyrite and pyrite. This is a replacement deposit: you can see the sulphide replacing the rock from the outside inwards."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-cowley", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "measure", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide065_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Geological cross-section with drill holes through zones of epidote plus albite, quartz plus pyrite, quartz plus sericite and quartz plus sericite plus chlorite"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide065_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark green drill core with pale green epidote patches", "cap": "1 · distal"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide066_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Drill core streaked with white quartz and dark pyrite stringers", "cap": "2 · ore zone"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide067_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale grey, fine-grained siliceous drill core", "cap": "3 · above the ore"}], "img": "img/L2/slide065_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · Cowley Hills VMS, near Woodlawn", "q": "Describe each core in one line. Then predict what happens to AI, sodium and barium as you go from 1 to 2 to 3.", "seen": "<ul><li><b>1:</b> dark basalt with patches of epidote, chlorite and albite. In thin section you can still see the original basalt texture. Pyroxene has changed to epidote and chlorite, and Ca-plagioclase has changed to albite. AI is about 50 and CCPI is about 90. Epidote with chlorite tells you that this rock was too hot and too far away from the ore.</li><li><b>2:</b> strong quartz and pyrite stringers, more than chlorite, with chalcopyrite. You can no longer recognise it as basalt. AI rises to about 85, CCPI is about 95, and Na<sub>2</sub>O falls to 0.14%. This is because sodium is mobile in hot fluid and is removed.</li><li><b>3:</b> strong quartz and sericite alteration, just above the ore. The rock is mostly quartz with a lot of muscovite. Barium (754 ppm) and copper (687 ppm) are high, even though the rock is not ore. This is because in VMS systems barium is deposited after the sulphides, higher in the sequence.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">Down the drill hole, AI and CCPI fall a little and then rise sharply at the mineralisation. After the mineralisation they fall again. Sodium shows the opposite pattern. Also, if a unit that should be distal plots in the \"favourable\" footwall field, check it again. You may have interpreted it wrongly, and there may be undiscovered mineralisation nearby.</p>", "e": "Down the drill hole, AI and CCPI fall a little and then rise sharply at the mineralisation. After the mineralisation they fall again. Sodium shows the opposite pattern. Also, if a unit that should be distal plots in the \"favourable\" footwall field, check it again. You may have interpreted it wrongly, and there may be undiscovered mineralisation nearby."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-veins", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "vector", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide069_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark rock cut by a thin pale vein with only a narrow pale margin", "cap": "A · Sn-W vein"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide070_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark purple-brown rock with pale bleached halos along fractures", "cap": "B · bleaching"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide070_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark sulphide-rich core with angular pale bleached host rock fragments", "cap": "C · silver ore"}], "img": "img/L2/slide069_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · narrow alteration around veins", "q": "Why can you not use a large alteration halo to find veins like A? What does the pale rock in B and C tell you?", "seen": "<p>Vein A contains a lot of tin and tungsten, but its alteration zone is only a few centimetres wide. Hydrothermal vein deposits usually have very narrow alteration zones, one metre wide at most. So alteration cannot guide you to them from far away. But you do see <b>bleaching</b>. Acidic fluid removes iron and magnesium, and the silicon and aluminium that stay make the rock pale. Nothing was added; the rock is white because the colouring elements have gone. When you start to see bleaching like B, you are close to the vein. In C, the dark ore contains about 2000 ppm silver, and the host rock fragments inside it are completely bleached.</p>", "e": "Vein A contains a lot of tin and tungsten, but its alteration zone is only a few centimetres wide. Hydrothermal vein deposits usually have very narrow alteration zones, one metre wide at most. So alteration cannot guide you to them from far away. But you do see bleaching. Acidic fluid removes iron and magnesium, and the silicon and aluminium that stay make the rock pale. Nothing was added; the rock is white because the colouring elements have gone. When you start to see bleaching like B, you are close to the vein. In C, the dark ore contains about 2000 ppm silver, and the host rock fragments inside it are completely bleached."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-chert", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "sed", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide006_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Outcrop of pale angular chert fragments packed together", "cap": "A · outcrop"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide007_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Sliced slab with pale, grey and red chert clasts of different textures in a red matrix", "cap": "B · sawn slab"}], "img": "img/L3/slide006_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · chert breccia, outcrop and slab", "q": "The slab has clasts of several colours and textures, and a red matrix. Did the red colour form during the brecciation?", "seen": "<p>The different clast colours are different generations or textures of chert, which all replaced carbonate. The red colour is almost certainly hematite from later weathering and oxidation. It did not form during the brecciation. Always separate the event that made the texture from the later events that coloured it (Lecture 2).</p>", "e": "The different clast colours are different generations or textures of chert, which all replaced carbonate. The red colour is almost certainly hematite from later weathering and oxidation. It did not form during the brecciation. Always separate the event that made the texture from the later events that coloured it (Lecture 2)."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-quarry", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "sed", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide009_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Quarry face with labelled collapse breccia, in situ breccia pipe, collapse breccia pipe and stratiform breccia zones"}], "img": "img/L3/slide009_2.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · limestone quarry wall", "q": "Find the collapse features in this real quarry face. Which labelled body has collapsed beds that dip inwards? Where would you expect the flat base?", "seen": "<p>The collapse breccia pipes on the right are the V-shaped, chaotic bodies where roof rock fell in. The in situ breccia pipe is broken, but the pieces have hardly moved. At the base, the stratiform breccia marks the level of the dissolved layer: a flat, sharp floor.</p>", "e": "The collapse breccia pipes on the right are the V-shaped, chaotic bodies where roof rock fell in. The in situ breccia pipe is broken, but the pieces have hardly moved. At the base, the stratiform breccia marks the level of the dissolved layer: a flat, sharp floor."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-faultbx", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "fault", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide011_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Banded grey shale and sandstone cut by a vertical zone of broken rock", "cap": "A · bedded sequence"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide012_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale green angular blocks separated by thin dark seams", "cap": "B · close-up"}], "img": "img/L3/slide011_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · two fault breccias", "q": "In A, how do you know that the broken zone is a fault and not a sedimentary breccia? Classify B.", "seen": "<p>A: the broken zone is a narrow, steep band that cuts straight across the shale and sandstone beds. The beds on each side are not broken. This shape shows fault movement. B: the pale blocks are large and angular, and they still almost fit together. There are only thin seams of matrix between them. This is a crackle breccia, with very little rotation.</p>", "e": "A: the broken zone is a narrow, steep band that cuts straight across the shale and sandstone beds. The beds on each side are not broken. This shape shows fault movement. B: the pale blocks are large and angular, and they still almost fit together. There are only thin seams of matrix between them. This is a crackle breccia, with very little rotation."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-magbx", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "magvol", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide016_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Grey rock with pale subrounded andesite clasts", "cap": "A · Payandayan volcano, West Java"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide016_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Brown sulphide matrix with dark rounded clasts", "cap": "B · Sudbury, Canada"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide016_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Black chromitite fragments in bright green and white cement", "cap": "C · Coolac, NSW"}], "img": "img/L3/slide016_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · three magmatic breccias", "q": "In two of these breccias, the matrix was molten. Which two? What is the green cement in C?", "seen": "<ul><li><b>A:</b> subrounded andesite clasts, some with reaction rims. They broke while the magma was still partly liquid, and they reacted with it.</li><li><b>B:</b> well-rounded clasts of the host norite in a sulphide matrix. The sulphide was also a melt (a separate sulphide liquid), so the matrix was molten.</li><li><b>C:</b> chromitite that broke while it was still magmatic. Later fluids cemented it and grew green uvarovite (chromium garnet) and grossular.</li></ul><p class=\"so\">So A and B had molten matrices. The cement in C formed later, from fluids. Rounded clasts and reaction rims tell you the setting was hot and magmatic.</p>", "e": "So A and B had molten matrices. The cement in C formed later, from fluids. Rounded clasts and reaction rims tell you the setting was hot and magmatic."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-volbx", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "magvol", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide019_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Large dark boulder made of packed angular lava blocks", "cap": "A · block breccia"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide022_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Drill core of angular mixed rock fragments of many types", "cap": "B · Kulnura Quarry, NSW"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide021_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Boulder of pale and grey fragments in a fine ashy matrix", "cap": "C · pyroclastic breccia"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide023_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Outcrop with large rounded and angular boulders in a fine matrix, very poorly sorted", "cap": "D · debris flow"}], "img": "img/L3/slide019_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · volcanic breccias", "q": "Which breccia was formed by gravity, not heat? What is unusual about the clasts in B?", "seen": "<p><b>D</b> formed by gravity. It has a huge range of clast sizes and is very poorly sorted. It is a debris flow (epiclastic). <b>B</b> is a phreatic breccia. Lava met groundwater, and the steam explosion broke the country rock. The mixture was pushed upwards as a breccia dyke. This is why most of the clasts in B are country rock, not volcanic rock. A is a block breccia, formed when a lava flow broke itself. C is pyroclastic: many rock types in an ash matrix.</p>", "e": "D formed by gravity. It has a huge range of clast sizes and is very poorly sorted. It is a debris flow (epiclastic). B is a phreatic breccia. Lava met groundwater, and the steam explosion broke the country rock. The mixture was pushed upwards as a breccia dyke. This is why most of the clasts in B are country rock, not volcanic rock. A is a block breccia, formed when a lava flow broke itself. C is pyroclastic: many rock types in an ash matrix."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-hydbx", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "hydro", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide025_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pink granite fragments in white silica with purple fluorite", "cap": "A · Mount Surprise, QLD"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide025_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Grey granite clasts separated by brown sphalerite", "cap": "B · Aspasia mine, Georgetown"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide026_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Rounded dark clasts in white quartz with grey stibnite", "cap": "C · Hillgrove, NE NSW"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide026_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale altered granite clasts in a crushed granite matrix stained brown", "cap": "D · Mount Turner porphyry"}], "img": "img/L3/slide025_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · hydrothermal breccias from Queensland and NSW", "q": "In which of these is the cement itself ore? In D, what is the matrix made of?", "seen": "<ul><li><b>A:</b> granite broken by an explosion, then cemented by fine silica (chalcedony) and purple fluorite. The escaping gases and fluids carried these minerals.</li><li><b>B:</b> granite clasts cemented by sphalerite. The cement is the ore. You can see that the clasts are altered.</li><li><b>C:</b> quartz crystals grew around the clasts first. Then stibnite (antimony sulphide, the ore) filled the gaps.</li><li><b>D:</b> a milled-matrix breccia. Strongly altered granite clasts sit in granite that the fluid crushed to powder. The sulphides that were there have oxidised to goethite.</li></ul><p class=\"so\">B and C have ore as the cement. Some epithermal deposits are completely hosted in breccia, not veins. Kelian in Kalimantan is the classic example.</p>", "e": "B and C have ore as the cement. Some epithermal deposits are completely hosted in breccia, not veins. Kelian in Kalimantan is the classic example."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-banded", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "banded", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide034_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Brassy chalcopyrite with thin bronze pyrrhotite layers", "cap": "A · Jaguar, WA (VMS)"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide034_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale pyrite with darker sphalerite bands", "cap": "B · Scuddles, WA (VMS)"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide035_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Grey slab with dark and light sulphide bands that are gently folded", "cap": "C · Mount Isa, QLD (SEDEX)"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide035_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark slab with fine wavy pale bands", "cap": "D · Lady Loretta, QLD (SEDEX)"}], "img": "img/L3/slide034_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · four banded ores", "q": "The bands in C are folded. Does this mean that later tectonics deformed the ore?", "seen": "<p>Not necessarily. At Mount Isa, the galena-sphalerite bands and the shale between them are folded together in small soft-sediment folds. These folds formed by compaction while the mud was still wet. The sulphide and the shale behaved as one wet sediment, which is more evidence that they formed together. Tectonic folds would also fold later veins, and would have the same axial plane across the whole outcrop.</p>", "e": "Not necessarily. At Mount Isa, the galena-sphalerite bands and the shale between them are folded together in small soft-sediment folds. These folds formed by compaction while the mud was still wet. The sulphide and the shale behaved as one wet sediment, which is more evidence that they formed together. Tectonic folds would also fold later veins, and would have the same axial plane across the whole outcrop."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-lamext", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "veins", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide040_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "White quartz with many fine dark grey laminations", "cap": "A · Mount Turner porphyry, QLD"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide040_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale banded quartz and carbonate vein with fine dark bands", "cap": "B · Masuparia, Kalimantan"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide041_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Vein with quartz crystals grown straight across from wall to wall, with brassy sulphide", "cap": "C · Bingham, USA"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide041_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Sandstone with thin parallel rusty quartz veins", "cap": "D · Budgery mine, NSW"}], "img": "img/L3/slide040_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · laminated and extensional veins", "q": "Which two are crack-seal veins? In which direction do the crystals point in C?", "seen": "<p><b>A and B</b> are laminated crack-seal veins. They opened and sealed, again and again, many times. In A, the dark layers are molybdenite. B is a classic ginguro vein from an epithermal system, with bands of colourless quartz, off-white ankerite and black sulphide. That sample contained about 1500 g/t silver and 150 g/t gold. If you find ginguro banding while exploring an epithermal system, it is a very good sign. <b>C and D</b> are extensional veins. They are planar, and the crystals grew at right angles to the vein walls, because the rock simply pulled apart. In C, chalcopyrite, bornite and gold filled the vein later.</p>", "e": "A and B are laminated crack-seal veins. They opened and sealed, again and again, many times. In A, the dark layers are molybdenite. B is a classic ginguro vein from an epithermal system, with bands of colourless quartz, off-white ankerite and black sulphide. That sample contained about 1500 g/t silver and 150 g/t gold. If you find ginguro banding while exploring an epithermal system, it is a very good sign. C and D are extensional veins. They are planar, and the crystals grew at right angles to the vein walls, because the rock simply pulled apart. In C, chalcopyrite, bornite and gold filled the vein later."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-space", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "veins", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide043_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Grey rock with a vein whose centre is filled with dark grey molybdenite", "cap": "A · Bingham, molybdenite infill"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide043_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark euhedral sphalerite crystals with pale quartz filling around them", "cap": "B · Strauss open cut, Drake"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide045_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pink altered monzonite cut by many crossing grey and black veinlets", "cap": "C · Goonumbla, NSW"}], "img": "img/L3/slide043_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · space-fill and stockwork", "q": "A and B are both space-fill veins, but the minerals formed in the opposite order. Explain this. Then explain why companies like C so much.", "seen": "<p><b>A</b> has the usual order: quartz grew on the walls first, and molybdenite later filled the open centre. <b>B</b> has the opposite order: euhedral sphalerite crystals grew first into open space, and quartz later filled around them. You can tell because the sphalerite has crystal faces, and crystal faces only grow into open space. <b>C</b> is a stockwork: many crossing veins of early quartz and later quartz-chalcocite in brecciated, altered monzonite. Many veins mean many separate pulses of mineralisation, and this is what makes porphyries and epithermal systems large. Stockworks form by strong hydrofracturing, when fluid at very high pressure separates from magma close to the surface.</p>", "e": "A has the usual order: quartz grew on the walls first, and molybdenite later filled the open centre. B has the opposite order: euhedral sphalerite crystals grew first into open space, and quartz later filled around them. You can tell because the sphalerite has crystal faces, and crystal faces only grow into open space. C is a stockwork: many crossing veins of early quartz and later quartz-chalcocite in brecciated, altered monzonite. Many veins mean many separate pulses of mineralisation, and this is what makes porphyries and epithermal systems large. Stockworks form by strong hydrofracturing, when fluid at very high pressure separates from magma close to the surface."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-magvein", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "veins", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide038_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Olive-green dunite with a single black sinuous chromitite vein", "cap": "A · Kondyor Massif, Russia"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide046_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Grey phyllite with a large pale quartz vein on the left and a cross-cutting shear vein", "cap": "B · Tallebung Sn-W, NSW"}], "img": "img/L3/slide038_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · magmatic and complex veins", "q": "A is not hydrothermal at all. How did a chromitite vein form in dunite? What is the sequence of events in B?", "seen": "<p><b>A</b> is a magmatic vein. Dense chromite crystals separated from the melt and collected together, forming a vein of only one mineral inside the silicate host. Magmatic veins are rare. The usual examples are chromitite in dunite, and pyrrhotite-pentlandite in ultramafic rock. <b>B</b> records two events. First, extension opened a large quartz-cassiterite vein. Later, shearing formed a shear vein that cuts across it.</p>", "e": "A is a magmatic vein. Dense chromite crystals separated from the melt and collected together, forming a vein of only one mineral inside the silicate host. Magmatic veins are rare. The usual examples are chromitite in dunite, and pyrrhotite-pentlandite in ultramafic rock. B records two events. First, extension opened a large quartz-cassiterite vein. Later, shearing formed a shear vein that cuts across it."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-meta", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "meta", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide048_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Coarse, shiny grey galena and sphalerite crystals", "cap": "A · Hera, level 615"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide048_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Galena with long, ribbon-like stretched crystals", "cap": "B · Woodcutters, NT"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide049_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Banded rock of dark pyrrhotite-rich bands and pale quartz bands", "cap": "C · Mount Windarra, WA"}], "img": "img/L3/slide048_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · metamorphosed ores", "q": "What does each sample tell you about what metamorphism did?", "seen": "<ul><li><b>A:</b> very coarse, recrystallised galena-sphalerite. The coarse grain size shows remobilisation.</li><li><b>B:</b> strongly strained galena, stretched into long ribbons during deformation. Galena flows in a ductile way.</li><li><b>C:</b> this was a banded iron formation of hematite and chert. During metamorphism, sulphur was added, so the hematite changed to pyrrhotite and the chert recrystallised to coarse quartz. The banding survived, but the minerals changed.</li></ul>", "e": "A: very coarse, recrystallised galena-sphalerite. The coarse grain size shows remobilisation. B: strongly strained galena, stretched into long ribbons during deformation. Galena flows in a ductile way. C: this was a banded iron formation of hematite and chert. During metamorphism, sulphur was added, so the hematite changed to pyrrhotite and the chert recrystallised to coarse quartz. The banding survived, but the minerals changed."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-durch", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "meta", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide051_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground face with pale angular siltstone clasts in grey fine-grained sulphide", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide055_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground face with clasts of quartz and orange garnet in fine grey galena", "cap": "B"}], "img": "img/L3/slide051_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · durchbewegung at Hera", "q": "B has orange garnet in quartz clasts, inside fine galena. Why is it surprising to find garnet in a sulphide vein? What does it tell you?", "seen": "<p>Garnet needs high temperature. These quartz-garnet clasts formed deeper in the deposit, in hotter skarn conditions. Later they were broken off and carried up a shear vein inside ductile, fine-grained galena. So this one sample records three things: an early hot event, a later deformation, and galena flowing around the broken pieces. A shows the same texture, with bleached siltstone clasts in sphalerite-galena.</p>", "e": "Garnet needs high temperature. These quartz-garnet clasts formed deeper in the deposit, in hotter skarn conditions. Later they were broken off and carried up a shear vein inside ductile, fine-grained galena. So this one sample records three things: an early hot event, a later deformation, and galena flowing around the broken pieces. A shows the same texture, with bleached siltstone clasts in sphalerite-galena."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-gold", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "cobar", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide062_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Close-up of honey-coloured sphalerite with a red marker circle around a tiny gold grain"}], "img": "img/L3/slide062_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · find the gold", "q": "The red circle shows visible gold at Federation. What mineral is the gold in? Why is this useful to know when you log core?", "seen": "<p>The gold is in honey-coloured sphalerite with very little iron, and the sphalerite shows almost no deformation. At Federation, gold usually occurs with this honey sphalerite and with quartz-sphalerite-galena veins. So when you log core and see honey sphalerite, look more carefully with a hand lens. Deposits often have local zones of high strain or of a particular mineral assemblage. This is why you look at every scale.</p>", "e": "The gold is in honey-coloured sphalerite with very little iron, and the sphalerite shows almost no deformation. At Federation, gold usually occurs with this honey sphalerite and with quartz-sphalerite-galena veins. So when you log core and see honey sphalerite, look more carefully with a hand lens. Deposits often have local zones of high strain or of a particular mineral assemblage. This is why you look at every scale."},
 {"id": "L4-obs-greys", "kind": "obs", "lec": 4, "sec": "scope", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L4/slide012_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Leica research microscope with a camera mounted on top", "cap": "A · dual transmitted and reflected light"}, {"src": "img/L4/slide013_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Computer screen showing a live grey photomicrograph", "cap": "B · live camera view"}], "img": "img/L4/slide012_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 4 field task · the UNSW research microscope", "q": "The screen in B looks almost completely grey. What is the biggest practical difficulty for beginners in reflected light?", "seen": "<p>Everything is a shade of grey, white or pale yellow, and the differences are small. You will get used to it. Two tips. First, work in a dark room if possible, so your eyes can adjust (microscopists in the past used a completely dark room). Second, always compare a grain with the grains next to it, because how bright a grain looks and what colour it seems depend on its neighbours. Pyrite looks bright next to sphalerite, but clearly duller next to galena.</p>", "e": "Everything is a shade of grey, white or pale yellow, and the differences are small. You will get used to it. Two tips. First, work in a dark room if possible, so your eyes can adjust (microscopists in the past used a completely dark room). Second, always compare a grain with the grains next to it, because how bright a grain looks and what colour it seems depend on its neighbours. Pyrite looks bright next to sphalerite, but clearly duller next to galena."},
 {"id": "L4-obs-covsph", "kind": "obs", "lec": 4, "sec": "props", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L4/slide007_1.jpg", "mic": true, "alt": "Covellite in PPL at no rotation: patchy pale and deep blue"}, {"src": "img/L4/slide007_2.jpg", "mic": true, "alt": "Covellite rotated 45 degrees: the blues have changed intensity and tone"}, {"src": "img/L4/slide008_1.jpg", "mic": true, "alt": "Sphalerite under crossed polars with bright yellow and orange spots inside dark grains"}], "img": "img/L4/slide007_1.jpg", "mic": true, "lbl": "Lecture 4 field task · covellite and sphalerite", "q": "The first two photos show the same covellite grains, rotated 45°. Name the property. The third photo is sphalerite under crossed polars. Where do the coloured spots come from?", "seen": "<p>Covellite shows very strong <b>bireflectance pleochroism</b>: when you rotate the stage, the blue changes in both colour and brightness. No other common mineral looks like this, so once you have seen it, you will always recognise covellite. The sphalerite shows <b>internal reflections</b>. Light enters the partly transparent grain, reflects off fractures inside it, and comes back as yellow, orange and red flashes. In sphalerite, this property is diagnostic.</p>", "e": "Covellite shows very strong bireflectance pleochroism: when you rotate the stage, the blue changes in both colour and brightness. No other common mineral looks like this, so once you have seen it, you will always recognise covellite. The sphalerite shows internal reflections. Light enters the partly transparent grain, reflects off fractures inside it, and comes back as yellow, orange and red flashes. In sphalerite, this property is diagnostic."},
 {"id": "L4-obs-timing", "kind": "obs", "lec": 4, "sec": "textures", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L4/slide016_1.jpg", "mic": true, "alt": "Pale yellow pyrite grains with embayed edges, surrounded by darker grey sphalerite and bright galena"}, {"src": "img/L4/slide017_1.jpg", "mic": true, "alt": "A smooth, lobed bright galena inclusion inside grey sphalerite"}, {"src": "img/L4/slide018_1.jpg", "mic": true, "alt": "A pyrite grain with ragged, eaten-away margins filled by other sulphides"}], "img": "img/L4/slide016_1.jpg", "mic": true, "lbl": "Lecture 4 field task · reading timing from texture", "q": "In each photo, which mineral formed first? One photo shows two minerals that grew at the same time. Which one, and how can you tell?", "seen": "<ul><li><b>Left:</b> pyrite formed first, and its edges have been partly dissolved. Then sphalerite (darker grey) formed, and then galena (bright white). The paragenesis is pyrite, sphalerite, galena, and you can read it all in one field of view.</li><li><b>Middle:</b> a galena inclusion in sphalerite. The boundaries are smooth and rounded, and each mineral pushes into the other (cuspate-lobate boundaries). Neither mineral cuts the other, so they crystallised at the same time. This is an equilibrium texture.</li><li><b>Right:</b> caries (or atoll) texture. Pyrite was partly dissolved and replaced by a later sulphide, like a tooth with holes.</li></ul>", "e": "Left: pyrite formed first, and its edges have been partly dissolved. Then sphalerite (darker grey) formed, and then galena (bright white). The paragenesis is pyrite, sphalerite, galena, and you can read it all in one field of view. Middle: a galena inclusion in sphalerite. The boundaries are smooth and rounded, and each mineral pushes into the other (cuspate-lobate boundaries). Neither mineral cuts the other, so they crystallised at the same time. This is an equilibrium texture. Right: caries (or atoll) texture. Pyrite was partly dissolved and replaced by a later sulphide, like a tooth with holes."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-cast", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "cast", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide001_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Outcrop of dark, finely layered sheared rock with a pale band and a scale card", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide001_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale outcrop with tight, closely spaced folds", "cap": "B"}], "img": "img/L5/slide001_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · two outcrops", "q": "Name the structure in each photo. Which one would be the better pathway for fluid?", "seen": "<p><b>A</b> is a shear zone: strongly flattened, foliated rock with a strong fabric. <b>B</b> shows very tight, almost isoclinal folds in metasediments. Shear zones are the classic pathway for fluid, especially at the brittle-ductile transition, because repeated movement keeps opening the rock again. Fold hinges can also trap fluid, as you will see later. Both are important for orogenic gold.</p>", "e": "A is a shear zone: strongly flattened, foliated rock with a strong fabric. B shows very tight, almost isoclinal folds in metasediments. Shear zones are the classic pathway for fluid, especially at the brittle-ductile transition, because repeated movement keeps opening the rock again. Fold hinges can also trap fluid, as you will see later. Both are important for orogenic gold."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-bowdens", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "cast", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide005_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Core with mixed angular and rounded clasts of several rock types", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide005_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Core packed with pale clasts of varied size", "cap": "B"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide005_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Core with an orange altered top and a dark fine breccia below", "cap": "C"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide005_4.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Orange core cut by a network of dark filled fractures", "cap": "D"}], "img": "img/L5/slide005_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · breccias at Bowdens, NSW", "q": "Bowdens is an epithermal silver deposit. Use what you learned in Lecture 3. Which of these is a jig-saw breccia? Which one formed from fluid under high pressure?", "seen": "<p><b>D</b> is the jig-saw breccia. The orange clasts still fit together, with dark filling between them. It formed only by fracturing and dilation. <b>A and B</b> are polymictic breccias with many types of clasts. One of them comes from an epiclastic deposit, like an avalanche from the wall of the caldera, formed by gravity. <b>C</b> is a rock-flour breccia. Hydrothermal fluid under high pressure ground the rock so finely that it became black. When you describe clasts, say whether they are monomictic (one type) or polymictic (many types), angular or rounded, and sorted or not sorted.</p>", "e": "D is the jig-saw breccia. The orange clasts still fit together, with dark filling between them. It formed only by fracturing and dilation. A and B are polymictic breccias with many types of clasts. One of them comes from an epiclastic deposit, like an avalanche from the wall of the caldera, formed by gravity. C is a rock-flour breccia. Hydrothermal fluid under high pressure ground the rock so finely that it became black. When you describe clasts, say whether they are monomictic (one type) or polymictic (many types), angular or rounded, and sorted or not sorted."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-rylstone", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "cast", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide008_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Road cutting with pale and orange layered volcanic ash beds and a person for scale"}], "img": "img/L5/slide008_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · Rylstone Volcanics, NSW", "q": "These are ash beds with sandstone beds between them. Why are some layers more altered, and set back further into the cutting, than others?", "seen": "<p>The fine-grained ash beds are more altered, more weathered and more fractured. The coarser sandstone stands out. Fine ash reacts more easily, and the contacts between beds are planes of weakness. So fluid mainly flowed along certain layers and into them. Bedding was a structure that already existed, and it controlled where the alteration went.</p>", "e": "The fine-grained ash beds are more altered, more weathered and more fractured. The coarser sandstone stands out. Fine ash reacts more easily, and the contacts between beds are planes of weakness. So fluid mainly flowed along certain layers and into them. Bedding was a structure that already existed, and it controlled where the alteration went."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-phases", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "scale", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide022_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Five block diagrams showing rift, spreading, island arc, closing and collision phases"}], "img": "img/L5/slide022_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · the life of a basin", "q": "Match each phase to the deposits that form in it: rift, spreading, island arc, closing.", "seen": "<ul><li><b>Rift:</b> the rift itself forms little ore. But carbonatites from deep in the mantle can rise through continental rifts (rare earth elements).</li><li><b>Spreading:</b> thin crust and melting caused by lower pressure (decompression melting): VMS and magmatic sulphide deposits.</li><li><b>Island arc:</b> subduction magmas: porphyry and epithermal deposits.</li><li><b>Closing:</b> collision and metamorphism: orogenic gold and many other deposits.</li></ul><p class=\"so\">When the crust is pulled apart, it becomes thinner. Magma rises because of decompression, and fluids find easy pathways. This is why extension is important for so many deposit types.</p>", "e": "When the crust is pulled apart, it becomes thinner. Magma rises because of decompression, and fluids find easy pathways. This is why extension is important for so many deposit types."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-nswfaults", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "scale", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide014_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Map of central NSW with many coloured fault lines, mostly trending north-south, with named faults listed"}], "img": "img/L5/slide014_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · faults of central NSW", "q": "Most of the major faults run roughly north-south. Why? How could you use this map to search for deposits?", "seen": "<p>Eastern Australia grew by accretion. New terranes were added from the east onto the craton in the west, so the main sutures and faults run roughly north-south. Deposits are often found where major faults cross smaller cross-faults. In the late 1970s and 1980s, Ed O'Driscoll, a geologist at Western Mining Corporation, chose exploration targets where lines (lineaments) crossed on satellite images. His ideas are seen as part of the path to the discovery of Olympic Dam. A large deposit needs a lot of fluid, and a lot of fluid needs a large structure.</p>", "e": "Eastern Australia grew by accretion. New terranes were added from the east onto the craton in the west, so the main sutures and faults run roughly north-south. Deposits are often found where major faults cross smaller cross-faults. In the late 1970s and 1980s, Ed O'Driscoll, a geologist at Western Mining Corporation, chose exploration targets where lines (lineaments) crossed on satellite images. His ideas are seen as part of the path to the discovery of Olympic Dam. A large deposit needs a lot of fluid, and a lot of fluid needs a large structure."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-extcomp", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "styles", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide020_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Two block diagrams: extension with meteoric water flowing down a fault into a single orebody, and compression with fluid squeezed up into several smaller orebodies"}], "img": "img/L5/slide020_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · same rocks, different stress", "q": "Compare the top diagram (extension) with the bottom diagram (compression). How does the shape of the ore body change, and why?", "seen": "<p>In extension, the fault opens a pathway. Meteoric water flows down through limestone and mixes with basin fluid, and one large lens of ore forms on the fault. In compression, the fold and the fault act as an aquitard (a barrier). Fluid is pushed up underneath it and replaces favourable beds, so a series of smaller lenses forms. The rocks are the same, but the stress is different, so the shape of the ore is completely different. Also, if there is compression in one part of an orogen, there is extension in another part.</p>", "e": "In extension, the fault opens a pathway. Meteoric water flows down through limestone and mixes with basin fluid, and one large lens of ore forms on the fault. In compression, the fold and the fault act as an aquitard (a barrier). Fluid is pushed up underneath it and replaces favourable beds, so a series of smaller lenses forms. The rocks are the same, but the stress is different, so the shape of the ore is completely different. Also, if there is compression in one part of an orogen, there is extension in another part."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-carlin", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "styles", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide023_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Diagram of Carlin-type gold: fluid rising up a fault and spreading laterally into silty limestone beds, with jasperoid and decalcification"}], "img": "img/L5/slide023_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · Carlin-type gold", "q": "Why does the gold spread sideways at certain levels, instead of staying on the fault?", "seen": "<p>The extensional fault is the pathway, and the silty limestone beds absorb the fluid like a sponge. Fluid rises up the fault and spreads sideways into favourable beds that react easily. It dissolves carbonate (decalcification) and replaces it with silica (jasperoid). The gold grains are micron-sized and invisible, so you explore for the alteration and the right host beds. Carlin deposits in Nevada have produced tens of millions of ounces of gold, and important deposits of this type are now known in southern China. Without extension, there would be no deposit.</p>", "e": "The extensional fault is the pathway, and the silty limestone beds absorb the fluid like a sponge. Fluid rises up the fault and spreads sideways into favourable beds that react easily. It dissolves carbonate (decalcification) and replaces it with silica (jasperoid). The gold grains are micron-sized and invisible, so you explore for the alteration and the right host beds. Carlin deposits in Nevada have produced tens of millions of ounces of gold, and important deposits of this type are now known in southern China. Without extension, there would be no deposit."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-enechelon", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "styles", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide027_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Block diagram of strike-slip deformation with synthetic en echelon faults"}], "img": "img/L5/slide027_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · strike-slip", "q": "Strike-slip movement forms stepped, S-shaped (sigmoidal) en echelon veins. In the goldfields of the Lachlan Fold Belt, are these the veins that contain gold?", "seen": "<p>Usually not. In the Lachlan Fold Belt, the sigmoidal en echelon veins are early D1 structures. They crystallised during movement (syn-deformational, rootless veins), and they usually contain no gold. The gold-bearing veins are much flatter and formed later. This is the timing idea again: know which generation of veins contains the gold.</p>", "e": "Usually not. In the Lachlan Fold Belt, the sigmoidal en echelon veins are early D1 structures. They crystallised during movement (syn-deformational, rootless veins), and they usually contain no gold. The gold-bearing veins are much flatter and formed later. This is the timing idea again: know which generation of veins contains the gold."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-zagros", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "traps", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide026_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Map of the Zagros fold-thrust belt between the Arabian and Iranian plates with oil fields along anticlines"}], "img": "img/L5/slide026_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · the Zagros fold belt", "q": "The Arabian plate pushing into Iran formed a fold-thrust belt with many very large anticlines. Why does the oil not escape up the many faults?", "seen": "<p>There are two seals. First, shale layers in the fold hinges are impermeable, so oil that rises into the anticline is trapped under them. Second, fault movement grinds rock into clay gouge, and oil and gas cannot flow through clay. So many faults act as barriers, not as leaks. Structures control energy resources in the same way that they control metals.</p>", "e": "There are two seals. First, shale layers in the fold hinges are impermeable, so oil that rises into the anticline is trapped under them. Second, fault movement grinds rock into clay gouge, and oil and gas cannot flow through clay. So many faults act as barriers, not as leaks. Structures control energy resources in the same way that they control metals."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-orofold", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "traps", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide025_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Cross-section of orogenic gold formation with metamorphic fluids rising along faults into fold hinges and saddle reefs"}], "img": "img/L5/slide025_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · orogenic gold in folds", "q": "Find the three places where gold can be trapped in this compressional system.", "seen": "<p>Metamorphic fluids form at depth, where pyrrhotite changes to pyrite and releases arsenic, antimony and H<sub>2</sub>S. These fluids rise along faults and collect in three places. (1) <b>Fold hinges</b>, which are zones of weakness. (2) <b>Saddle reefs</b>, where a small amount of extension during folding opens gaps between beds at the hinge, as at Bendigo and Ballarat. (3) <b>Replacement</b> of reactive units, such as limestone or banded iron formation, at the top of the fold.</p>", "e": "Metamorphic fluids form at depth, where pyrrhotite changes to pyrite and releases arsenic, antimony and H 2 S. These fluids rise along faults and collect in three places. (1) Fold hinges, which are zones of weakness. (2) Saddle reefs, where a small amount of extension during folding opens gaps between beds at the hinge, as at Bendigo and Ballarat. (3) Replacement of reactive units, such as limestone or banded iron formation, at the top of the fold."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-superpit", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "yilgarn", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide028_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Aerial view of the huge terraced Superpit beside the town of Kalgoorlie", "cap": "A · the Superpit, Golden Mile"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide028_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Close-up of a quartz vein in dark rock with visible gold", "cap": "B · the target"}], "img": "img/L5/slide028_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · the Kalgoorlie Superpit", "q": "The pit is long and narrow, several kilometres long. What controls this shape? What does B tell you about why visible gold is rare here?", "seen": "<p>The pit follows a structural corridor: a group of lodes hosted in shear zones. So the pit is long in the direction of strike. It has been mined since the 1890s, and as the pit grew, it removed the old underground tunnels. B shows a quartz vein with gold. But on the Golden Mile, most gold is fine-grained and invisible, so a sample like B is unusual. Kalgoorlie is one of the richest gold fields in the world. <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording</span></p>", "e": "The pit follows a structural corridor: a group of lodes hosted in shear zones. So the pit is long in the direction of strike. It has been mined since the 1890s, and as the pit grew, it removed the old underground tunnels. B shows a quartz vein with gold. But on the Golden Mile, most gold is fine-grained and invisible, so a sample like B is unusual. Kalgoorlie is one of the richest gold fields in the world."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-redlake", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "yilgarn", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide037_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Geological map of the Red Lake greenstone belt with the Campbell-Red Lake mine marked (22.1 million ounces)", "cap": "A · map"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide037_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "3D view of the Red Lake mine zones plunging steeply with depth", "cap": "B · mine model"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide037_4.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground face with flat-lying quartz veins and a miner", "cap": "C · underground"}], "img": "img/L5/slide037_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · Red Lake, Canada", "q": "Red Lake in the Superior Craton has produced about 22 million ounces of gold. Compare the overall shape of the orebody in B with the veins in C. What is typical here?", "seen": "<p>The whole deposit plunges steeply to a great depth, but the individual quartz veins and lenses in C are almost horizontal. Steeply plunging ore shoots made of flat veins are very typical of orogenic gold. These systems go very deep. With today's high gold prices, it is now profitable to mine deep levels that were not profitable a few years ago, even though deep mining is expensive (for example, the air must be cooled).</p>", "e": "The whole deposit plunges steeply to a great depth, but the individual quartz veins and lenses in C are almost horizontal. Steeply plunging ore shoots made of flat veins are very typical of orogenic gold. These systems go very deep. With today's high gold prices, it is now profitable to mine deep levels that were not profitable a few years ago, even though deep mining is expensive (for example, the air must be cooled)."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-granny", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "yilgarn", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide039_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Geological map of the Granny Smith area with a granite, porphyry dykes, banded iron formation and mineralised structures along one side of the granite"}], "img": "img/L5/slide039_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · Granny Smith, Yilgarn", "q": "The same granite and the same tectonic setting occur in other parts of the belt, with no deposit. Here, all the mineralisation is on one side of the granite margin. Why only one side?", "seen": "<p>The granite acted as a rheological barrier. It is a strong, rigid body that fluid could not pass through. So fluid collected and deposited gold along the structures against one side of the granite. Local structure decides whether a deposit forms, not the regional setting. This is why two areas that look the same at regional scale can be very different. Porphyry dykes and banded iron formation also help to concentrate ore here.</p>", "e": "The granite acted as a rheological barrier. It is a strong, rigid body that fluid could not pass through. So fluid collected and deposited gold along the structures against one side of the granite. Local structure decides whether a deposit forms, not the regional setting. This is why two areas that look the same at regional scale can be very different. Porphyry dykes and banded iron formation also help to concentrate ore here."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-continuum", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "yilgarn", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide033_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Schematic crustal profile showing gold deposits from sub-greenschist to granulite facies along one shear system"}], "img": "img/L5/slide033_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · the crustal continuum", "q": "One shear system, many depths. What is the main idea?", "seen": "<p>This is the crustal continuum model. The same type of fluid system can deposit gold at many depths along one connected set of pathways, from deep, hot granulite facies up to shallow sub-greenschist facies. The style of the deposit changes with depth: ductile shear-hosted ore at depth, and brittle veins and breccias at shallow levels. But it is one system. The largest Yilgarn deposits, including Kalgoorlie, are in the upper greenschist part of the profile.</p>", "e": "This is the crustal continuum model. The same type of fluid system can deposit gold at many depths along one connected set of pathways, from deep, hot granulite facies up to shallow sub-greenschist facies. The style of the deposit changes with depth: ductile shear-hosted ore at depth, and brittle veins and breccias at shallow levels. But it is one system. The largest Yilgarn deposits, including Kalgoorlie, are in the upper greenschist part of the profile."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-source", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "yilgarn", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide031_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Two people with a very large gold-bearing specimen on a pallet"}], "img": "img/L5/slide031_3.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · where did the gold come from?", "q": "A specimen like this makes you ask: where did all this gold come from?", "seen": "<p>There is no single answer. There were several gold events of different ages, and probably several sources. (1) Granites: gold is concentrated in the last melts and fluids of the granite. (2) Basalts: fluid collects small amounts of gold from very large volumes of basalt and carries it to shear zones. (3) An original contribution from the mantle. Here is a good example of timing: at some nickel mines, gold is found in the same structures as the nickel. But the gold came in during a later orogenic event, long after the nickel ore formed with its host rocks.</p>", "e": "There is no single answer. There were several gold events of different ages, and probably several sources. (1) Granites: gold is concentrated in the last melts and fluids of the granite. (2) Basalts: fluid collects small amounts of gold from very large volumes of basalt and carries it to shear zones. (3) An original contribution from the mantle. Here is a good example of timing: at some nickel mines, gold is found in the same structures as the nickel. But the gold came in during a later orogenic event, long after the nickel ore formed with its host rocks."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-pilbara", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "pilbara", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide046_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Geologist standing among tall, vertical, pencil-like rock columns", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide045_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Diagram of a diapiric dome with flattening on top and constriction on the sides", "cap": "B"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide049_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Old mine adit tunnel cut into pale weathered rock", "cap": "C · Warrawoona"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide049_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Quartz veins in sheared rock with a pen for scale", "cap": "D"}], "img": "img/L5/slide046_2.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · Pilbara outcrops", "q": "What are the \"pencils\" in A? How does B explain them?", "seen": "<p>A shows vertical L-tectonites. The rock was stretched straight down into rods as the greenstone sank between the rising domes. B shows the strain pattern of a rising dome: flattening on top, constriction (squeezing) on the sides, and folds with possible L-tectonites in the hinge zones. Gold is also found in the zone where the greenstone sank. C is an old mine tunnel at Warrawoona: the old miners followed the steep shear zone. D shows the quartz veins in that sheared rock.</p>", "e": "A shows vertical L-tectonites. The rock was stretched straight down into rods as the greenstone sank between the rising domes. B shows the strain pattern of a rising dome: flattening on top, constriction (squeezing) on the sides, and folds with possible L-tectonites in the hinge zones. Gold is also found in the zone where the greenstone sank. C is an old mine tunnel at Warrawoona: the old miners followed the steep shear zone. D shows the quartz veins in that sheared rock."},
 {"id": "L6-obs-pillows", "kind": "obs", "lec": 6, "sec": "dive1977", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L6/slide007_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A submersible's sampling arm reaching towards a smoking chimney on the dark seafloor", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L6/slide008_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Rounded, bulbous dark lava forms on the seafloor", "cap": "B"}], "img": "img/L6/slide007_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 6 field task · on the seafloor", "q": "A shows a sample being taken. B is lava on the floor of the rise. What kind of lava is it? What would its surface look like in hand specimen?", "seen": "<p><b>A</b> shows the submersible's arm breaking a sample off an active black smoker, so the minerals can be studied while they form. <b>B</b> is young, still-hot pillow lava. Lava that erupts into 2 °C water cools instantly, so the rims are glassy. A sample from the active Brothers volcano shows this clearly: it is very glassy, because it froze as soon as it touched the cold water.</p>", "e": "A shows the submersible's arm breaking a sample off an active black smoker, so the minerals can be studied while they form. B is young, still-hot pillow lava. Lava that erupts into 2 °C water cools instantly, so the rims are glassy. A sample from the active Brothers volcano shows this clearly: it is very glassy, because it froze as soon as it touched the cold water."},
 {"id": "L6-obs-smokers", "kind": "obs", "lec": 6, "sec": "smoker", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L6/slide038_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Two vents side by side: on the left pale cloudy fluid, on the right a dense black plume"}], "img": "img/L6/slide038_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 6 field task · two vents", "q": "Which vent is hotter? What is each vent putting into the water?", "seen": "<p>The vent on the right is a <b>black smoker</b>. It is hotter (350 to 400 °C), and dissolved metals are forming tiny dark sulphide particles. The vent on the left is a <b>white smoker</b>. It is cooler (260 to 300 °C) and gives off pale barium, calcium and silicon minerals, such as baryte, anhydrite and amorphous silica. The sulphide ore comes from the black smokers, not the white smokers.</p>", "e": "The vent on the right is a black smoker. It is hotter (350 to 400 °C), and dissolved metals are forming tiny dark sulphide particles. The vent on the left is a white smoker. It is cooler (260 to 300 °C) and gives off pale barium, calcium and silicon minerals, such as baryte, anhydrite and amorphous silica. The sulphide ore comes from the black smokers, not the white smokers."},
 {"id": "L6-obs-chimney", "kind": "obs", "lec": 6, "sec": "chimney", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L6/slide023_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A broken black smoker chimney fragment labelled chalcopyrite, sphalerite, pyrite and anhydrite across its thickness"}], "img": "img/L6/slide023_2.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 6 field task · a real chimney, cut open", "q": "The labels go across the thickness of this fragment. Which side faced the hot central channel? Which side faced the seawater?", "seen": "<p>The <b>chalcopyrite</b> side lined the hot channel, because copper minerals need the hottest fluid. Moving outwards, the minerals form at lower and lower temperatures: sphalerite and pyrite, and then <b>anhydrite</b> on the outer wall, where heated seawater deposited calcium sulphate. Read every chimney in the same way: copper inside, zinc and iron in the middle, sulphate outside.</p>", "e": "The chalcopyrite side lined the hot channel, because copper minerals need the hottest fluid. Moving outwards, the minerals form at lower and lower temperatures: sphalerite and pyrite, and then anhydrite on the outer wall, where heated seawater deposited calcium sulphate. Read every chimney in the same way: copper inside, zinc and iron in the middle, sulphate outside."},
 {"id": "L6-obs-fauna", "kind": "obs", "lec": 6, "sec": "life", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L6/slide019_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "White tubes with red plumes: giant tube worms", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L6/slide019_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A pale long-legged crab on dark rock", "cap": "B"}, {"src": "img/L6/slide019_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pink shrimp crowded on pale mineral-coated rock", "cap": "C"}], "img": "img/L6/slide019_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 6 field task · vent fauna", "q": "None of these animals can use sunlight at 2 km depth. What is at the base of their food chain?", "seen": "<p><b>Chemistry, not light.</b> Microbes use the energy in the vent chemicals (especially sulphide) to make organic matter. The tube worms (A) keep these microbes inside their bodies. Crabs (B) and prawns (C) feed and hunt in the same food web. These organisms are called extremophiles.</p>", "e": "Chemistry, not light. Microbes use the energy in the vent chemicals (especially sulphide) to make organic matter. The tube worms (A) keep these microbes inside their bodies. Crabs (B) and prawns (C) feed and hunt in the same food web. These organisms are called extremophiles."},
 {"id": "L6-obs-claw", "kind": "obs", "lec": 6, "sec": "find", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L6/slide026_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A robotic claw gripping the side of a chimney", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L6/slide026_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A claw placing a rusty chimney piece into a numbered sample tray", "cap": "B"}], "img": "img/L6/slide026_2.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 6 field task · rock sampling", "q": "Why does each sample go into its own numbered container?", "seen": "<p>The claw breaks the chimney off and drops it into a numbered container. The position of the vehicle is known, so every sample is linked to an exact place on the seafloor. Then the minerals and chemistry can be mapped. This data is expensive: a research voyage costs several million dollars, and one towed sensor system lost after the Hunga Tonga eruption was worth about $10 million.</p>", "e": "The claw breaks the chimney off and drops it into a numbered container. The position of the vehicle is known, so every sample is linked to an exact place on the seafloor. Then the minerals and chemistry can be mapped. This data is expensive: a research voyage costs several million dollars, and one towed sensor system lost after the Hunga Tonga eruption was worth about $10 million."},
 {"id": "L6-obs-brothers", "kind": "obs", "lec": 6, "sec": "kermadec", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L6/slide035_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Tall chimney specimen with orange-brown and grey patches", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L6/slide035_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Grey and dark chimney specimen", "cap": "B"}, {"src": "img/L6/slide035_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Seafloor view of two smoking chimneys labelled twin beehives", "cap": "C"}, {"src": "img/L6/slide036_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Several orange and grey chimneys on the seafloor at Brothers", "cap": "D"}], "img": "img/L6/slide035_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 6 field task · Brothers NW caldera", "q": "A is a copper-rich chimney and B is a zinc-rich chimney, both from the NW caldera. C and D show chimneys in place. What different conditions do Cu-rich and Zn-rich chimneys record? Why might one chimney be both?", "seen": "<p>A <b>Cu-rich</b> chimney grew from <b>high-temperature</b> fluid (chalcopyrite forms at about 350 °C). A <b>Zn-rich</b> chimney grew from <b>cooler</b> fluid (sphalerite, galena). One chimney can change along its length. In one example, the side grew at high temperature, but the very top grew at a lower temperature, with sphalerite and pyrite. Activity changes all the time: in D, some chimneys are active, and others have broken off and stopped. C is the \"twin beehives\" site, where a copper-rich chimney was sampled.</p>", "e": "A Cu-rich chimney grew from high-temperature fluid (chalcopyrite forms at about 350 °C). A Zn-rich chimney grew from cooler fluid (sphalerite, galena). One chimney can change along its length. In one example, the side grew at high temperature, but the very top grew at a lower temperature, with sphalerite and pyrite. Activity changes all the time: in D, some chimneys are active, and others have broken off and stopped. C is the \"twin beehives\" site, where a copper-rich chimney was sampled."},
 {"id": "L7-obs-spinifex", "kind": "obs", "lec": 7, "sec": "komatiite", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L7/slide001_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Green rock with long, thin, criss-crossing dark blades"}], "img": "img/L7/slide001_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 7 field task · a komatiite flow top", "q": "Name the texture. What does it tell you about how this lava cooled?", "seen": "<p>This is <b>spinifex texture</b>: long, thin, blade-shaped crystals (originally olivine) that cross each other, like the spinifex grass of the WA outback. It forms when ultramafic lava cools very fast, in the thin sheet flows and at the tops of flows. The rock is green because metamorphism changed the olivine to serpentine. This is why the host belts are called greenstone belts.</p>", "e": "This is spinifex texture: long, thin, blade-shaped crystals (originally olivine) that cross each other, like the spinifex grass of the WA outback. It forms when ultramafic lava cools very fast, in the thin sheet flows and at the tops of flows. The rock is green because metamorphism changed the olivine to serpentine. This is why the host belts are called greenstone belts."},
 {"id": "L7-obs-lefroy", "kind": "obs", "lec": 7, "sec": "kambalda", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L7/slide025_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Flat, pale blue expanse of a salt lake with small dark islands", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L7/slide026_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Aerial view of orange open-cut pits in flat red country", "cap": "B"}], "img": "img/L7/slide025_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 7 field task · the setting", "q": "A is Lake Lefroy, a salt lake. One mine goes under it. B shows the Kambalda open cuts in the 1970s. What problem does the salt lake cause when you mine sulphide ore under it?", "seen": "<p>Salt water and oxidising sulphides damage equipment. In that mine, equipment lasts no more than about four years, because everything rusts and the oxidising sulphides make sulphuric acid. This makes mining under the lake very expensive. Open cuts (B) were a cheap way to start when the ore was close to the surface.</p>", "e": "Salt water and oxidising sulphides damage equipment. In that mine, equipment lasts no more than about four years, because everything rusts and the oxidising sulphides make sulphuric acid. This makes mining under the lake very expensive. Open cuts (B) were a cheap way to start when the ore was close to the surface."},
 {"id": "L7-obs-kamb-ore", "kind": "obs", "lec": 7, "sec": "kambalda", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L7/slide030_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground wall of layered pale and dark rock", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L7/slide030_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark rounded pillow shapes with bright metallic material filling the gaps, a hammer for scale", "cap": "B"}], "img": "img/L7/slide030_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 7 field task · underground at Kambalda", "q": "A and B are both ore. Describe how the sulphide occurs in each one.", "seen": "<p><b>A</b> is a banded, nickel-rich cumulate: layers with disseminated sulphides that settled out together with the crystals. <b>B</b> is sulphide that fills the spaces between basalt pillows: the dense sulphide liquid flowed down into gaps in the footwall. Both are segregation. B shows how easily the sulphide liquid moved.</p>", "e": "A is a banded, nickel-rich cumulate: layers with disseminated sulphides that settled out together with the crystals. B is sulphide that fills the spaces between basalt pillows: the dense sulphide liquid flowed down into gaps in the footwall. Both are segregation. B shows how easily the sulphide liquid moved."},
 {"id": "L8-obs-shatter", "kind": "obs", "lec": 8, "sec": "sudbury", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L8/slide007_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A hand-held rock with fine, fan-like striations across its surface"}], "img": "img/L8/slide007_2.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 8 field task · a hand specimen from Sudbury", "q": "The lines spread out across the surface like a fan. What kind of structure is this? What does it tell you about Sudbury?", "seen": "<p>This looks like a <b>shatter cone</b>: cone-shaped fracture surfaces with lines on them, made by the shock wave of a very fast impact. The excellent shatter cones around Sudbury are part of the evidence that the basin is an impact structure. Normal igneous or tectonic processes do not make them.</p>", "e": "This looks like a shatter cone: cone-shaped fracture surfaces with lines on them, made by the shock wave of a very fast impact. The excellent shatter cones around Sudbury are part of the evidence that the basin is an impact structure. Normal igneous or tectonic processes do not make them."},
 {"id": "L8-obs-monche", "kind": "obs", "lec": 8, "sec": "norilsk", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L8/slide015_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A geologist standing on a hill above a barren, smoky industrial landscape with a smelter", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L8/slide016_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Grey outcrop with pale veins and a hammer", "cap": "B"}, {"src": "img/L8/slide016_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale weathered rock surface studded with dark, protruding crystals and a hammer", "cap": "C"}, {"src": "img/L8/slide016_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A brassy, metallic boulder with a hammer", "cap": "D"}], "img": "img/L8/slide015_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 8 field task · Monchegorsk, Arctic Russia", "q": "In A, a geologist stands above a town where nickel has been smelted since the 1930s. In C, the dark crystals stand out from a pale surface. Why? What is D?", "seen": "<p>Many years of smelting have badly polluted Monchegorsk. There are few plants, there is sulphuric acid in the air, and a freshwater lake about 50 km away has a pH of 3. For a geologist, there is one good result: the outcrop is excellent, because nothing grows on it. In C, acid has dissolved the <b>plagioclase</b> along grain boundaries, so the resistant <b>orthopyroxene</b> crystals of the gabbronorite stand out. D is <b>massive sulphide ore</b>, mainly pyrrhotite with pentlandite and chalcopyrite.</p>", "e": "Many years of smelting have badly polluted Monchegorsk. There are few plants, there is sulphuric acid in the air, and a freshwater lake about 50 km away has a pH of 3. For a geologist, there is one good result: the outcrop is excellent, because nothing grows on it. In C, acid has dissolved the plagioclase along grain boundaries, so the resistant orthopyroxene crystals of the gabbronorite stand out. D is massive sulphide ore, mainly pyrrhotite with pentlandite and chalcopyrite."},
 {"id": "L8-obs-layers", "kind": "obs", "lec": 8, "sec": "bushveld", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L8/slide037_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Outcrop of alternating black and white layers", "cap": "A · Bushveld"}, {"src": "img/L8/slide038_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Boulder with parallel dark and grey bands and a hammer", "cap": "B · Sopche, Monchegorsk"}], "img": "img/L8/slide037_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 8 field task · layered rocks", "q": "Name the black layers and the white layers in A. Explain why they alternate.", "seen": "<p>The black layers are <b>chromitite</b> and the white layers are <b>anorthosite</b>. Each new batch of magma crystallised chromite, which sank, and plagioclase, which floated. This made a chromitite layer, then anorthosite, then the next batch. It looks like sedimentary bedding, and it also forms by a physical process. B is layered chromitite at Sopche, which looks more like a layered intrusion than a pod.</p>", "e": "The black layers are chromitite and the white layers are anorthosite. Each new batch of magma crystallised chromite, which sank, and plagioclase, which floated. This made a chromitite layer, then anorthosite, then the next batch. It looks like sedimentary bedding, and it also forms by a physical process. B is layered chromitite at Sopche, which looks more like a layered intrusion than a pod."},
 {"id": "L8-obs-coolac", "kind": "obs", "lec": 8, "sec": "podiform", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L8/slide039_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Grassy hills of the Coolac Serpentinite Belt", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L8/slide039_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Weathered rock with parallel raised ribs", "cap": "B"}, {"src": "img/L8/slide039_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale rock with a dark vein-like streak and a red pen", "cap": "C"}, {"src": "img/L8/slide039_4.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale rock studded with dark rounded nodules and a hammer", "cap": "D"}], "img": "img/L8/slide039_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 8 field task · Coolac Serpentinite Belt, southern NSW", "q": "Name the chromite texture in D. What do C and B show?", "seen": "<p><b>D</b> is <b>nodular chromitite</b>: rounded chromite nodules in pale dunite, typical of podiform deposits. <b>C</b> is chromitite that has collected in a vein shape. <b>B</b> is primary layering in the host harzburgite: olivine-rich and orthopyroxene-rich layers that alternate, and weather out as ridges. A is the landscape of the serpentinite belt.</p>", "e": "D is nodular chromitite: rounded chromite nodules in pale dunite, typical of podiform deposits. C is chromitite that has collected in a vein shape. B is primary layering in the host harzburgite: olivine-rich and orthopyroxene-rich layers that alternate, and weather out as ridges. A is the landscape of the serpentinite belt."},
 {"id": "L8-obs-khibiny", "kind": "obs", "lec": 8, "sec": "phos", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L8/slide042_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Large grey open-cut mine in treeless hills", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L8/slide042_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "White rock with sprays of dark needle-like crystals", "cap": "B"}, {"src": "img/L8/slide042_4.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Rock with golden-brown radiating bladed crystals labelled astrophyllite", "cap": "C"}], "img": "img/L8/slide042_2.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 8 field task · Khibiny massif, Arctic Circle", "q": "A is an apatite mine. How do the rocks in B and C tell you that this is an alkaline intrusion?", "seen": "<p>Khibiny is a round, very alkaline intrusion, rich in sodium, potassium, fluorine and CO₂. Its minerals show this: dark sprays of Na-rich pyroxene and amphibole (B), and golden <b>astrophyllite</b> (C), an alkaline silicate with Ti. Normal gabbros and granites do not contain these minerals. The open cut (A) mines apatite, with rare earth elements as a by-product.</p>", "e": "Khibiny is a round, very alkaline intrusion, rich in sodium, potassium, fluorine and CO₂. Its minerals show this: dark sprays of Na-rich pyroxene and amphibole (B), and golden astrophyllite (C), an alkaline silicate with Ti. Normal gabbros and granites do not contain these minerals. The open cut (A) mines apatite, with rare earth elements as a by-product."},
 {"id": "L8-obs-shev", "kind": "obs", "lec": 8, "sec": "laterite", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L8/slide044_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Orange and brown pit wall of weathered serpentinite labelled with nickel grades of 1.88, 1.80 and 1.47 percent"}], "img": "img/L8/slide044_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 8 field task · Shevchenko deposit, Urals", "q": "The face is weathered serpentinite with grades of 1.47 to 1.88% Ni. What was this rock before weathering? Why is it ore now?", "seen": "<p>It was an <b>ultramafic rock</b>, serpentinised peridotite or dunite, with only about 0.2 to 0.4% Ni in its olivine. Strong weathering removed the magnesium and silica and left the nickel. So the nickel became several times more concentrated. It is attached to fine goethite and in clays. No sulphide was ever involved.</p>", "e": "It was an ultramafic rock, serpentinised peridotite or dunite, with only about 0.2 to 0.4% Ni in its olivine. Strong weathering removed the magnesium and silica and left the nickel. So the nickel became several times more concentrated. It is attached to fine goethite and in clays. No sulphide was ever involved."},
 {"id": "L9-obs-slab", "kind": "obs", "lec": 9, "sec": "what", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L9/bedded.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Cut slab of finely layered green-grey rock with pale and dark bands, one thicker speckled grey band across the middle, 2 cm scale bar"}], "img": "img/L9/bedded.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 9 field task · a slab of SEDEX ore", "q": "Before you read anything, describe the layering. How thick are the layers? How continuous are they? Are they all made of the same material?", "seen": "<ul><li>The layers are millimetres thick, very flat, and continuous across the 10 cm slab. There is only gentle wavy disturbance.</li><li>There are three kinds of band: thin pale layers, thin dark layers, and one thicker, speckled grey band with a sharp base that becomes finer upwards.</li><li>No veins cut the layers. There is no breccia and no lump of massive sulphide.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So this is sediment that was deposited layer by layer. The thick graded band is a turbidite. The dark layers are black shale, and the thin layers between them are sphalerite and galena. The sulphides were deposited together with the mud, layer by layer. This is the main observation behind the syngenetic model.</p>", "e": "So this is sediment that was deposited layer by layer. The thick graded band is a turbidite. The dark layers are black shale, and the thin layers between them are sphalerite and galena. The sulphides were deposited together with the mud, layer by layer. This is the main observation behind the syngenetic model."},
 {"id": "L9-obs-core", "kind": "obs", "lec": 9, "sec": "mcarthur", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L9/core.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Rows of shiny dark grey to black drill core with fine, slightly wavy laminations, a few broken intervals and one pale buff section"}], "img": "img/L9/core.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 9 field task · drill core of the host rock", "q": "What colour is the rock? What is its grain size? What does this tell you about the water it was deposited in?", "seen": "<ul><li>Black to dark grey, very fine grained, with fine continuous layers. There is no sign of burrows that mixed the layers.</li><li>One short pale interval, probably a bed with more carbonate.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So this is the typical host rock: organic-rich dolomitic siltstone. Black means the organic carbon was preserved. Fine layers that no animal disturbed (non-bioturbated) mean the bottom water had no oxygen. This is the anoxic basin floor with H₂S that is needed. Almost every SEDEX deposit is in rock like this. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 45</span></p>", "e": "So this is the typical host rock: organic-rich dolomitic siltstone. Black means the organic carbon was preserved. Fine layers that no animal disturbed (non-bioturbated) mean the bottom water had no oxygen. This is the anoxic basin floor with H₂S that is needed. Almost every SEDEX deposit is in rock like this."},
 {"id": "L9-obs-redbed", "kind": "obs", "lec": 9, "sec": "chem", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L9/redbed.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Outcrop of red-brown, finely bedded sandstone with a geological hammer for scale", "cap": "A · outcrop, hammer for scale"}, {"src": "img/L9/hemqtz.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Thin section of rounded clear quartz grains, each outlined by a thin dark rim, with dark material between grains", "cap": "B · thin section of the same rock"}], "img": "img/L9/redbed.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 9 field task · the aquifer in the McArthur Basin", "q": "What gives the sandstone its colour? What does this tell you about the brine that moved through it?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: red-brown, well-bedded sandstone.</li><li>B: rounded quartz grains, each with a thin dark coating. The coating is hematite.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So the colour comes from hematite (oxidised iron) coating every grain. The brine that moved through this aquifer was oxidised, which fits the pattern in northern Australia. In a reduced basin like the one at Sullivan, the grains would be coated with pyrite instead.</p>", "e": "So the colour comes from hematite (oxidised iron) coating every grain. The brine that moved through this aquifer was oxidised, which fits the pattern in northern Australia. In a reduced basin like the one at Sullivan, the grains would be coated with pyrite instead."},
 {"id": "L9-obs-micro", "kind": "obs", "lec": 9, "sec": "layers", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L9/sphlayer.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Thin section with a pale vertical band of fine grains between dark fine-grained bands, and rounded pale clusters scattered in the dark band to its right", "cap": "A · thin section"}, {"src": "img/L9/micro830.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Thin section with dark fine matrix and wavy pale bands, one band thickened over a lump, 830 micrometre scale bar", "cap": "B · thin section, scale bar 830 µm"}], "img": "img/L9/sphlayer.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 9 field task · under the microscope", "q": "At this scale, does the ore look like veins that cut the rock, or layers in it? How thick are the pale bands?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: a band of fine sphalerite grains parallel to the layering, with rounded clumps of sphalerite scattered in the darker sediment next to it.</li><li>B: pale sulphide bands, less than a millimetre thick, between dark carbonaceous mud layers. They curve gently over a lump.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So the sulphide is layered with the sediment; it does not cut it. There are very thin layers (microlaminae) of sulphide between layers of carbonaceous mudstone. The rounded clumps match sphalerite that grew in the water or brine and then sank. Notice how fine-grained everything is. This is why these ores are hard to process.</p>", "e": "So the sulphide is layered with the sediment; it does not cut it. There are very thin layers (microlaminae) of sulphide between layers of carbonaceous mudstone. The rounded clumps match sphalerite that grew in the water or brine and then sank. Notice how fine-grained everything is. This is why these ores are hard to process."},
 {"id": "L9-obs-breccia", "kind": "obs", "lec": 9, "sec": "anatomy", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L9/breccia.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Cut slab: the lower third is finely laminated grey and pale ore, the upper part is a breccia of angular to rounded clasts of many colours in a dark matrix, fining up into dark sand; a thin white vein cuts both; 2 cm scale"}], "img": "img/L9/breccia.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 9 field task · a slab from near a vent", "q": "Describe the two parts of this slab and the contact between them. What happened, and in what order?", "seen": "<ul><li>Bottom: finely laminated ore, with gently wavy layers and an irregular top that looks slightly eroded.</li><li>Top: a sedimentary breccia of angular to rounded clasts of different rocks. It becomes finer upwards into dark sand.</li><li>A thin white vein cuts both parts, so it formed last.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So the laminated Zn-Pb ore formed first. Then a debris flow, probably from an active fault scarp, deposited breccia on top of it. Later, a vein cut through everything. Breccia on top of banded sulphide is unusual. Breccias like this show the vent area, where fault movement keeps producing broken rock. Mineralising fluid that passes through a breccia near a vent will mineralise it too.</p>", "e": "So the laminated Zn-Pb ore formed first. Then a debris flow, probably from an active fault scarp, deposited breccia on top of it. Later, a vein cut through everything. Breccia on top of banded sulphide is unusual. Breccias like this show the vent area, where fault movement keeps producing broken rock. Mineralising fluid that passes through a breccia near a vent will mineralise it too."},
 {"id": "L9-obs-isa", "kind": "obs", "lec": 9, "sec": "halo", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L9/rl1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Reflected light view of wavy, very fine dark and white laminae with a white band and grey patches below, 1 mm scale bar", "cap": "A · reflected light, scale bar 1 mm"}, {"src": "img/L9/rl2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Reflected light view of a row of pale cream euhedral grains in a pale bluish-grey matrix between dark fine-grained laminae, 1 mm scale bar", "cap": "B · reflected light, scale bar 1 mm"}], "img": "img/L9/rl1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 9 field task · Mount Isa ore in reflected light", "q": "In B, the pale cream grains have straight edges and look pitted. Which mineral are they? What in A tells you that this is SEDEX ore?", "seen": "<ul><li>B: pale cream, high reflectance, hard (they stand higher and have pits from polishing), with crystal faces. These are euhedral <b>pyrite</b> grains, in a paler bluish-grey sulphide that is most likely galena.</li><li>A: very fine, wavy layers of sulphide and dark carbonaceous sediment, millimetres thick, gently folded.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So you have fine-grained laminated sulphide with pyrite in carbonaceous sediment. This is typical of SEDEX. The galena in B is identified only from colour and reflectance, so treat it as likely, not certain. The gentle folds in A could be soft-sediment folds or compaction, not later deformation.</p>", "e": "So you have fine-grained laminated sulphide with pyrite in carbonaceous sediment. This is typical of SEDEX. The galena in B is identified only from colour and reflectance, so treat it as likely, not certain. The gentle folds in A could be soft-sediment folds or compaction, not later deformation."},
 {"id": "L9-obs-galena", "kind": "obs", "lec": 9, "sec": "halo", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L9/rl4.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Reflected light view of a bright white mineral with sharp black triangular pits, enclosing a cream-coloured grain, set in grey material, 200 micrometre scale bar"}], "img": "img/L9/rl4.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 9 field task · a second north Australian ore", "q": "Look at the bright white mineral. What are the black triangles, and what mineral do they identify?", "seen": "<ul><li>Bright white, high reflectance, soft-looking surface.</li><li>Sharp black triangular pits.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it is <b>galena</b>. The triangles are cleavage pits, where cubic cleavage pieces were pulled out during polishing. The grey around it, with lower reflectance, is most likely sphalerite and carbonate gangue. The slide does not identify the cream grain inside it. From its colour alone, it could be pyrite.</p>", "e": "So it is galena. The triangles are cleavage pits, where cubic cleavage pieces were pulled out during polishing. The grey around it, with lower reflectance, is most likely sphalerite and carbonate gangue. The slide does not identify the cream grain inside it. From its colour alone, it could be pyrite."},
 {"id": "L9-obs-hemi", "kind": "obs", "lec": 9, "sec": "world", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L9/hemimorphite.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Close-up of brown, weathered, cavernous outcrop with white crusts of bladed crystals, and a dark lizard on the right"}], "img": "img/L9/hemimorphite.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 9 field task · the HYC discovery outcrop", "q": "This weathered outcrop was the only sign of HYC at the surface. What is the white mineral, and why is it important?", "seen": "<ul><li>Brown, iron-stained rock with many holes: a small gossan.</li><li>White crusts of fine, blade-shaped crystals.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">The white mineral is hemimorphite, Zn₄Si₂O₇(OH)₂·H₂O, a zinc silicate that forms when zinc sulphide weathers (the supergene process from Lecture 2). A very small gossan with a little hemimorphite was the only sign at the surface of what became Australia's largest SEDEX deposit. Syd Carter and Ron Beresford recognised it. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 46</span></p>", "e": "The white mineral is hemimorphite, Zn₄Si₂O₇(OH)₂·H₂O, a zinc silicate that forms when zinc sulphide weathers (the supergene process from Lecture 2). A very small gossan with a little hemimorphite was the only sign at the surface of what became Australia's largest SEDEX deposit. Syd Carter and Ron Beresford recognised it."},
 {"id": "L10-dcCards-0", "kind": "core", "lec": 10, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L10/ug-felsic.jpg", "lbl": "Decline face", "q": "What is this?", "o": ["Footwall felsic volcanics", "Hematite-flooded felsic clasts", "Chalcopyrite stringer zone", "Hanging-wall graphitic black shale", "Black shale over pillow basalt"], "a": 0, "e": "Flattened felsic clasts in the footwall. (slide 50)"},
 {"id": "L10-dcCards-1", "kind": "core", "lec": 10, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L10/ug-hematite.jpg", "lbl": "Decline face", "q": "What is this?", "o": ["Footwall felsic volcanics", "Hematite-flooded felsic clasts", "Chalcopyrite stringer zone", "Hanging-wall graphitic black shale", "Black shale over pillow basalt"], "a": 1, "e": "Hydrothermal hematite flooding felsic clasts near the ore: oxidised fluid, not weathering. (slide 51)"},
 {"id": "L10-dcCards-2", "kind": "core", "lec": 10, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L10/ug-stringer.jpg", "lbl": "Decline face", "q": "What is this?", "o": ["Footwall felsic volcanics", "Hematite-flooded felsic clasts", "Chalcopyrite stringer zone", "Hanging-wall graphitic black shale", "Black shale over pillow basalt"], "a": 2, "e": "Chalcopyrite-rich stringer ore in basalt, below the main lode. (slide 57)"},
 {"id": "L10-dcCards-3", "kind": "core", "lec": 10, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L10/ug-shale.jpg", "lbl": "Decline face", "q": "What is this?", "o": ["Footwall felsic volcanics", "Hematite-flooded felsic clasts", "Chalcopyrite stringer zone", "Hanging-wall graphitic black shale", "Black shale over pillow basalt"], "a": 3, "e": "Graphitic black shale with pyrite: soft and unstable when you mine through it. (slide 55)"},
 {"id": "L10-dcCards-4", "kind": "core", "lec": 10, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L10/ug-shalepillow.jpg", "lbl": "Decline face", "q": "What is this?", "o": ["Footwall felsic volcanics", "Hematite-flooded felsic clasts", "Chalcopyrite stringer zone", "Hanging-wall graphitic black shale", "Black shale over pillow basalt"], "a": 4, "e": "Black shale above pillow basalts. (slide 52)"},
 {"id": "L10-obs-hosts", "kind": "obs", "lec": 10, "sec": "settings", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L10/basalt.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark, very fine-grained rock with a few small white spots", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L10/rhyolite.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale cream, porous, fine-grained rock with many small holes", "cap": "B"}], "img": "img/L10/basalt.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 10 field task · two host rocks", "q": "Describe the colour, grain size and texture. Which rock would host VMS ore at a mid-ocean ridge, and which in an island arc?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: dark, very fine grained, with a few small amygdales or phenocrysts. A mafic volcanic rock: <b>basalt</b>.</li><li>B: pale, fine grained, full of small holes (vesicles). A felsic volcanic rock: <b>rhyolite</b>, here pumice-like.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So A is the host rock at spreading ridges and rifts. B is the host rock in arcs and back arcs, where the largest deposits are. In Australia, most VMS deposits have rhyolites in the footwall and basalts in the hanging wall. <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 6 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So A is the host rock at spreading ridges and rifts. B is the host rock in arcs and back arcs, where the largest deposits are. In Australia, most VMS deposits have rhyolites in the footwall and basalts in the hanging wall."},
 {"id": "L10-obs-culode", "kind": "obs", "lec": 10, "sec": "alteration", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L10/ug-culode.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground wall with rounded, dark green pillow shapes, some pale and bleached, with sulphide between them, under mesh"}], "img": "img/L10/ug-culode.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 10 field task · a copper-rich lode in the decline", "q": "You can still see the pillow shapes. What has happened to them? Why is this stronger in some places than in others?", "seen": "<ul><li>The pillow outlines are preserved, but some pillows are bleached pale and others are dark green.</li><li>Sulphide fills the spaces between pillows and replaces parts of them.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So hydrothermal fluid has replaced the basalt. Where the fluid flow was strongest, the pillows are bleached and replaced by chalcopyrite and pyrite. Where less fluid passed, there is only strong chlorite around the pillow edges. The strength of alteration shows where the old fluid pathways were. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 46</span></p>", "e": "So hydrothermal fluid has replaced the basalt. Where the fluid flow was strongest, the pillows are bleached and replaced by chalcopyrite and pyrite. Where less fluid passed, there is only strong chlorite around the pillow edges. The strength of alteration shows where the old fluid pathways were."},
 {"id": "L10-obs-pillows", "kind": "obs", "lec": 10, "sec": "styles", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L10/pillows.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A person standing on a hillside made entirely of stacked rounded and tube-shaped lava bodies"}], "img": "img/L10/pillows.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 10 field task · an ophiolite outcrop", "q": "What are these rounded shapes? What do they tell you about where this rock formed?", "seen": "<ul><li>Rounded to tube-shaped bodies about a metre across, stacked on top of each other. Each has a smooth skin, and the gaps between them are filled.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">These are <b>pillow lavas</b>: basalt that erupted underwater. Each blob cools quickly and forms a skin, and then the next blob grows out of it. They show that this was the seafloor. These are in Oman, in an ophiolite, which is oceanic crust pushed up onto the continent. This is where Cyprus-type deposits form. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 34</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 6 Oct</span></p>", "e": "These are pillow lavas: basalt that erupted underwater. Each blob cools quickly and forms a skin, and then the next blob grows out of it. They show that this was the seafloor. These are in Oman, in an ophiolite, which is oceanic crust pushed up onto the continent. This is where Cyprus-type deposits form."},
 {"id": "L10-obs-banded", "kind": "obs", "lec": 10, "sec": "jaguar", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L10/ug-banded.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Close view of a wall of grey sulphide with wavy pale and darker bands, a lens cap for scale"}], "img": "img/L10/ug-banded.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 10 field task · the main lode", "q": "From far away, this face looks like one massive block. Close up, what do you see? What might the bands record?", "seen": "<ul><li>Wavy, folded bands of slightly different colour and texture through the sulphide.</li><li>Very little galena: this ore is mostly pyrite, sphalerite and chalcopyrite.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So the \"massive\" sulphide is banded: some layers have more chalcopyrite, sphalerite or pyrite. Bands like these fit pulses of hotter and cooler fluid that overprinted each other, and were later deformed. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 58 and 61</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 6 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So the \"massive\" sulphide is banded: some layers have more chalcopyrite, sphalerite or pyrite. Bands like these fit pulses of hotter and cooler fluid that overprinted each other, and were later deformed."},
 {"id": "L11-vnCards-0", "kind": "core", "lec": 11, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L11/avein.jpg", "lbl": "Drill core", "q": "Irregular grey quartz veins with pink-orange edges. Which vein type?", "o": ["EB vein", "A vein", "B vein", "D vein"], "a": 1, "e": "A vein: sugary quartz with K-feldspar on its edges. (slide 26)"},
 {"id": "L11-vnCards-1", "kind": "core", "lec": 11, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L11/bvein.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "A straight, continuous vein with a centre line and no halo. Which type?", "o": ["EB vein", "A vein", "B vein", "D vein"], "a": 2, "e": "B vein: planar, with quartz that grew inwards from both walls to a centre line. These veins contain most of the ore. (slide 27)"},
 {"id": "L11-vnCards-2", "kind": "core", "lec": 11, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L11/dvein.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Dark veins rich in sulphide in pale, bleached rock where the feldspar has gone. Which type?", "o": ["EB vein", "A vein", "B vein", "D vein"], "a": 3, "e": "D vein: rich in pyrite, with a sericite halo that destroys feldspar. (slide 28)"},
 {"id": "L11-vnCards-3", "kind": "core", "lec": 11, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L11/ebvein.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Thin dark veinlets of a black mica, with almost no quartz or sulphide. Which type?", "o": ["EB vein", "A vein", "B vein", "D vein"], "a": 0, "e": "EB vein: early biotite, formed deep. (slide 29)"},
 {"id": "L11-obs-porph", "kind": "obs", "lec": 11, "sec": "host", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L11/granodiorite.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Cut slab of an evenly speckled pink, white and black coarse-grained rock", "cap": "A · hand specimen"}, {"src": "img/L11/porphyry.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Close-up of a rock with larger pale pink rectangular crystals scattered through a fine-grained red, grey and black groundmass, one crystal labelled phenocryst", "cap": "B · hand specimen"}], "img": "img/L11/granodiorite.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 11 field task · what makes it a porphyry?", "q": "Compare the grain size in A and B. Which texture gives porphyry deposits their name? What does it tell you about how the magma cooled?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: evenly coarse grained, with all crystals about the same size: pink K-feldspar, white plagioclase and quartz, and black biotite or hornblende. An equigranular granitoid such as granodiorite.</li><li>B: large, well-formed feldspar crystals (phenocrysts) in a much finer groundmass.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So B shows <b>porphyritic</b> texture, which gives the deposits their name. It shows two cooling rates. Crystals grew slowly at depth. Then the magma rose to a shallow level, and the rest crystallised quickly. Porphyry deposits form at exactly this shallow, high level.</p>", "e": "So B shows porphyritic texture, which gives the deposits their name. It shows two cooling rates. Crystals grew slowly at depth. Then the magma rose to a shallow level, and the rest crystallised quickly. Porphyry deposits form at exactly this shallow, high level."},
 {"id": "L11-obs-potassic", "kind": "obs", "lec": 11, "sec": "alteration", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L11/potassic.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Four photos A to D: a dark rock cut by quartz veins with K-feldspar labelled along their margins; a pink rock with dark green patches labelled biotite; a green rock with dark veins; a purple-grey rock with a network of thin veins"}], "img": "img/L11/potassic.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 11 field task · potassic alteration", "q": "In A and B, what colour is the K-feldspar? What dark mineral is growing in B? Why does this colour suggest an oxidised magma?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: K-feldspar (Kfs) alteration along the edges of quartz veins, at Bethsaida, British Columbia.</li><li>B: widespread pink K-feldspar with patches of fine hydrothermal biotite (Bt), and quartz-molybdenite veinlets, at Red Mountain, Yukon.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">This is potassic alteration, which is always present in the core. The K-feldspar is strongly pink because it contains iron, which is a sign of an oxidised system. In many porphyries you will not see much K-feldspar. Secondary biotite is the more common sign of potassic alteration. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 52</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 9 Oct</span></p>", "e": "This is potassic alteration, which is always present in the core. The K-feldspar is strongly pink because it contains iron, which is a sign of an oxidised system. In many porphyries you will not see much K-feldspar. Secondary biotite is the more common sign of potassic alteration."},
 {"id": "L11-obs-propylitic", "kind": "obs", "lec": 11, "sec": "alteration", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L11/propylitic.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Six photos: four green-grey drill cores, one with crosscutting veins and one with brassy grains, an outcrop of green-grey andesite with a hammer, and a close-up of a yellow-green radiating mineral in a clast"}], "img": "img/L11/propylitic.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 11 field task · the outer halo", "q": "Look at f, the yellow-green radiating mineral. What is it? Which alteration zone does it show you are in?", "seen": "<ul><li>The cores and outcrop are green-grey: chlorite.</li><li>f: a coarse, radiating, pistachio-green mass that forms a nodule in an altered volcanic clast: epidote.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So you are in the <b>propylitic</b> zone: chlorite, epidote, albite and calcite, with magnetite or pyrite. It is always present and forms the outer halo. The first epidote marks the start of the propylitic zone. These samples are from Schaft Creek, BC. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 53</span> (Scott et al., 2008)</p>", "e": "So you are in the propylitic zone: chlorite, epidote, albite and calcite, with magnetite or pyrite. It is always present and forms the outer halo. The first epidote marks the start of the propylitic zone. These samples are from Schaft Creek, BC. (Scott et al., 2008)"},
 {"id": "L11-obs-ust", "kind": "obs", "lec": 11, "sec": "vectors", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L11/ust-hs.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Six cut slabs: wavy pink-orange and grey bands; contorted black tourmaline and white quartz bands; green and lilac patches with arrows; orange zigzag bands in grey; stacked pale bands with grey comb crystals growing from one side; and a finely mottled pale rock"}], "img": "img/L11/ust-hs.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 11 field task · which way is up?", "q": "In e (Umnu Hutul), the grey quartz crystals grow from one side of each band. Which way was the top of the intrusion?", "seen": "<ul><li>e: repeated pale bands. Each band has a flat side, and grey quartz combs with points that all face the same way.</li><li>a, b, d: wavy to contorted banding. b is altered to tourmaline.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">USTs grow <b>downwards</b> into the magma from the roof. So the flat side that each layer grows from faces up, towards the cupola, and the crystal points face down. This is easy to read in the straight and wavy types, but much harder in the contorted or brecciated types. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 33 to 35</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 9 Oct</span></p>", "e": "USTs grow downwards into the magma from the roof. So the flat side that each layer grows from faces up, towards the cupola, and the crystal points face down. This is easy to read in the straight and wavy types, but much harder in the contorted or brecciated types."},
 {"id": "L11-obs-aeromag", "kind": "obs", "lec": 11, "sec": "vectors", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L11/aeromag.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Colourful aeromagnetic map with red highs and blue-purple lows, a north-south fault line and small circles marking deposits"}], "img": "img/L11/aeromag.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 11 field task · an aeromagnetic map", "q": "Chuquicamata and El Abra are both on magnetic lows. Porphyry magmas are oxidised and rich in magnetite, so why is there a low?", "seen": "<ul><li>Reds are magnetic highs, and blues and purples are lows. The deposits (circles) are along a fault, on lows.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">The hydrothermal fluid that passed through destroyed the primary magnetite, so the rock lost its magnetism. You want a porphyry that started rich in magnetite and was then flushed by a lot of fluid, because the fluid brought the metal. A magnetic low inside a magnetic intrusion is a target. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 21</span> (Behn et al., 2001) <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 9 Oct</span></p>", "e": "The hydrothermal fluid that passed through destroyed the primary magnetite, so the rock lost its magnetism. You want a porphyry that started rich in magnetite and was then flushed by a lot of fluid, because the fluid brought the metal. A magnetic low inside a magnetic intrusion is a target. (Behn et al., 2001)"},
 {"id": "L11-obs-climax", "kind": "obs", "lec": 11, "sec": "moly", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L11/climaxore.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Small pale grey rock with dark grey streaks and patches and a thin grey vein cutting it"}], "img": "img/L11/climaxore.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 11 field task · Climax ore", "q": "This is ore. What would you look for to see the molybdenum?", "seen": "<ul><li>A pale rock rich in quartz (silicified rhyolite porphyry), with dark grey streaks and a thin quartz vein.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">Look for soft, lead-grey to bluish, platy <b>molybdenite</b> in the veinlets and scattered through the rock. Most ore is in stockwork veins, with a little disseminated ore, at only 0.1 to 0.45% MoS₂. So you will not see much. Deposits range from 50 to 1500 Mt. They are mined by open cut, stoping and block caving, mostly in western North America (and now Tibet). <span class=\"srcs\">slide 40</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 9 Oct</span></p>", "e": "Look for soft, lead-grey to bluish, platy molybdenite in the veinlets and scattered through the rock. Most ore is in stockwork veins, with a little disseminated ore, at only 0.1 to 0.45% MoS₂. So you will not see much. Deposits range from 50 to 1500 Mt. They are mined by open cut, stoping and block caving, mostly in western North America (and now Tibet)."},
 {"id": "L12-obs-wire", "kind": "obs", "lec": 12, "sec": "why", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L12/goldwire.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale yellow, wiry, branching gold on a grey quartz vein surface, a pen for scale"}], "img": "img/L12/goldwire.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 12 field task · a museum specimen", "q": "This is gold wire in quartz. What shape are the gold \"wires\" really made of? What does the pale colour suggest?", "seen": "<ul><li>Fine, branching, wire-shaped gold on a quartz vein: crystalline gold, typical of epithermal systems.</li><li>A pale, slightly greenish yellow, not deep yellow.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">The wires are actually <b>long, stacked cubes</b> of gold. The colour gives a rough idea of the composition: pale gold contains a lot of silver, and deep yellow gold is purer. Do not expect to see this in real ore: most gold is invisible. <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 12 Oct</span></p>", "e": "The wires are actually long, stacked cubes of gold. The colour gives a rough idea of the composition: pale gold contains a lot of silver, and deep yellow gold is purer. Do not expect to see this in real ore: most gold is invisible."},
 {"id": "L12-obs-spring", "kind": "obs", "lec": 12, "sec": "fluids", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L12/hotspring.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Steaming turquoise hot pool rimmed with orange and red-brown crusts, forest behind"}], "img": "img/L12/hotspring.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 12 field task · a hot spring", "q": "What is this pool the surface sign of? What might the orange crust around it contain?", "seen": "<ul><li>A steaming, clear turquoise pool: hot water rich in silica.</li><li>Bright orange to red-brown crusts on the edge.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">This is the top of an active epithermal (geothermal) system. The orange material is arsenic and antimony sulphide. In some New Zealand pools, these crusts contain hundreds of grams per tonne of gold. The Broadlands geothermal field deposits kilograms of gold every year inside its pipes. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 16</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 12 Oct</span></p>", "e": "This is the top of an active epithermal (geothermal) system. The orange material is arsenic and antimony sulphide. In some New Zealand pools, these crusts contain hundreds of grams per tonne of gold. The Broadlands geothermal field deposits kilograms of gold every year inside its pipes."},
 {"id": "L12-obs-hera", "kind": "obs", "lec": 12, "sec": "mineralogy", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L12/hera-gold.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Polished dark grey and brown-black rock with pale veinlets, small bright yellow specks scattered along a dark band, a cm scale card above"}], "img": "img/L12/hera-gold.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 12 field task · find the gold", "q": "Look along the dark brown band. What are the yellow specks, and how large are they?", "seen": "<ul><li>Bright, metallic, butter-yellow specks a few millimetres long. They are concentrated along a dark brown band rich in sulphide, and thin pale veinlets cut the grey host rock.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">This is <b>visible gold</b> in polymetallic ore from the Hera mine, central NSW. Gold is the only common mineral that is this yellow, very soft and does not tarnish. Pyrite and chalcopyrite are paler or more brassy, and much harder. Specks like these are rare in most ores, where the gold is invisible. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 36</span></p>", "e": "This is visible gold in polymetallic ore from the Hera mine, central NSW. Gold is the only common mineral that is this yellow, very soft and does not tarnish. Pyrite and chalcopyrite are paler or more brassy, and much harder. Specks like these are rare in most ores, where the gold is invisible."},
 {"id": "L12-obs-reef", "kind": "obs", "lec": 12, "sec": "wits", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L12/wits-outcrop.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Rusty orange-brown outcrop of a rock packed with rounded pale pebbles in a finer matrix"}], "img": "img/L12/wits-outcrop.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 12 field task · a reef outcrop", "q": "Describe the clasts: shape, sorting and composition. What kind of rock is this, and what deposited it?", "seen": "<ul><li>Well-rounded pebbles of fairly similar size, mostly pale vein quartz, packed closely in a finer, iron-stained matrix.</li><li>Faint layering, which suggests bedding.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">It is a quartz-pebble <b>conglomerate</b>. Rounded clasts mean long transport by rivers. Almost all the pebbles are of one kind (monomictic: vein quartz, chert, pyrite). This is strange, because the basin is on Archean granite and greenstone, but there are no granite or greenstone clasts. Gold and uraninite are in the matrix. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 41 to 45</span></p>", "e": "It is a quartz-pebble conglomerate. Rounded clasts mean long transport by rivers. Almost all the pebbles are of one kind (monomictic: vein quartz, chert, pyrite). This is strange, because the basin is on Archean granite and greenstone, but there are no granite or greenstone clasts. Gold and uraninite are in the matrix."},
 {"id": "L13-obs-stives", "kind": "obs", "lec": 13, "sec": "what", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L13/stives.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground face of white and grey quartz vein with a broad patch of yellow metallic grains, a hand holding a hammer for scale"}], "img": "img/L13/stives.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 13 field task · a quartz vein underground", "q": "Everything yellow here is gold. What is it in? How would an orogenic gold geologist describe this ore?", "seen": "<ul><li>Milky white quartz with dark, sheared wall rock on each side.</li><li>Many grains of free, visible gold close together in the quartz.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">This is the classic orogenic ore: <b>free gold in a quartz vein</b> along a structure, here at St Ives, Western Australia. It is extremely rich, and very unusual. Most orogenic ore is low grade, and the gold is invisible in the vein and the altered wall rock. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 1</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 13 Oct</span></p>", "e": "This is the classic orogenic ore: free gold in a quartz vein along a structure, here at St Ives, Western Australia. It is extremely rich, and very unusual. Most orogenic ore is low grade, and the gold is invisible in the vein and the altered wall rock."},
 {"id": "L13-obs-holtermann", "kind": "obs", "lec": 13, "sec": "hillend", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L13/holtermann.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Old photograph of a bearded man in a waistcoat standing beside a tall, slab-like specimen almost as high as his chest", "cap": "A · 1872"}, {"src": "img/L13/holtermann-cast.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A man beside a life-size gold-coloured cast of a large, lumpy specimen", "cap": "B · a life-size cast"}], "img": "img/L13/holtermann.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 13 field task · the biggest “nugget”", "q": "Holtermann's \"nugget\" contained 3000 oz of gold. Why do geologists say it was not really a nugget?", "seen": "<ul><li>A slab about as tall as a man, irregular and blocky, not rounded or worn by water.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">It is not a <b>nugget</b> (a lump of gold transported in a placer). It is a piece of <b>quartz vein full of pure gold</b>, found on 19 October 1872 at Hawkins Hill, Hill End. At 3000 oz (93.31 kg), it is the largest single mass of gold ever recorded. It would be worth at least US$15 million today. Bernhardt Holtermann was a shopkeeper. On the goldfields, the shopkeepers made the money. His fortune paid for a photographic record of the NSW goldfields that still exists. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 9</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 13 Oct</span></p>", "e": "It is not a nugget (a lump of gold transported in a placer). It is a piece of quartz vein full of pure gold, found on 19 October 1872 at Hawkins Hill, Hill End. At 3000 oz (93.31 kg), it is the largest single mass of gold ever recorded. It would be worth at least US$15 million today. Bernhardt Holtermann was a shopkeeper. On the goldfields, the shopkeepers made the money. His fortune paid for a photographic record of the NSW goldfields that still exists."},
 {"id": "L13-obs-stawell", "kind": "obs", "lec": 13, "sec": "stawell", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L13/stawell-vein.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground face of black carbonaceous shale with many thin white quartz veins, cut by a thick white quartz vein"}], "img": "img/L13/stawell-vein.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 13 field task · two generations of quartz", "q": "There are at least two sets of quartz here. Which came first? Which one probably contains the gold?", "seen": "<ul><li>Many thin, irregular, deformed white quartz veins in black carbonaceous shale.</li><li>A thicker quartz vein that cuts straight through them.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">The thin, deformed veins are earlier, and the thick vein that cuts them is later. This is a cross-cutting relationship. This is the mineralised vein of the Hanging Wall lode, cutting earlier barren quartz. In orogenic gold, the vein with gold is usually a late one, so it is important to know which generation you are looking at. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 25</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 13 Oct</span></p>", "e": "The thin, deformed veins are earlier, and the thick vein that cuts them is later. This is a cross-cutting relationship. This is the mineralised vein of the Hanging Wall lode, cutting earlier barren quartz. In orogenic gold, the vein with gold is usually a late one, so it is important to know which generation you are looking at."},
 {"id": "L13-obs-carlin", "kind": "obs", "lec": 13, "sec": "carlin", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L13/carlin-ore.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark grey, fine-grained rock with a rough surface and a few tiny glints"}], "img": "img/L13/carlin-ore.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 13 field task · Carlin ore", "q": "This is gold ore. Find the gold.", "seen": "<ul><li>A dark, fine-grained carbonaceous sedimentary rock with a little fine pyrite. No visible gold at all.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">You cannot, and that is the point. Carlin gold is so fine that it is almost invisible. It is locked in pyrite that contains arsenic. What you can see are the arsenic and antimony minerals that occur with it. The ore looks like waste, so Carlin mines must assay all the time to know that they are still in ore. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 31 and 33</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 13 Oct</span></p>", "e": "You cannot, and that is the point. Carlin gold is so fine that it is almost invisible. It is locked in pyrite that contains arsenic. What you can see are the arsenic and antimony minerals that occur with it. The ore looks like waste, so Carlin mines must assay all the time to know that they are still in ore."},
 {"id": "L13-obs-pyrite", "kind": "obs", "lec": 13, "sec": "carlin", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L13/carlin-pyrite.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Electron image of a cluster of fine pyrite among quartz crystals; a zoned pyrite grain with a speckled rim around a cleaner core and an arrow across it; and a graph of element counts along the arrow, higher in the rim"}], "img": "img/L13/carlin-pyrite.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 13 field task · where is the gold in the pyrite?", "q": "The laser moved across the zoned pyrite from the rim to the core. Where are gold and arsenic highest?", "seen": "<ul><li>A clean core and a spotted rim full of inclusions.</li><li>On the trace, gold, arsenic and the other trace elements jump up when the beam enters the rim. They are higher across the rim than in the core.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">The gold is in the <b>rim</b>. This is thin pyrite rich in arsenic, formed during the ore stage. It grew over earlier pyrite that had little gold, when the ore fluid cooled and mixed with heated meteoric water. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 34</span> (Muntean et al., 2011) <span class=\"srcs\">slide notes</span> <span style=\"color:var(--muted)\">The recording says the gold is in the core, but the figure and the slide notes put it in the rim. This page follows the slide.</span></p>", "e": "The gold is in the rim. This is thin pyrite rich in arsenic, formed during the ore stage. It grew over earlier pyrite that had little gold, when the ore fluid cooled and mixed with heated meteoric water. (Muntean et al., 2011) The recording says the gold is in the core, but the figure and the slide notes put it in the rim. This page follows the slide."},
 {"id": "L13-obs-tuena", "kind": "obs", "lec": 13, "sec": "tuena", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L13/tuena-veins.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground wall of grey-green schistose rock with pale, folded and broken quartz veins along the foliation"}], "img": "img/L13/tuena-veins.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 13 field task · veins in the adit", "q": "These pale quartz veins are parallel to the foliation and are folded. Did they form before or after the main deformation?", "seen": "<ul><li>Pale quartz in pods and lenses, folded and pulled into the foliation. Some have lost their connection to the rest of the vein (rootless).</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">These veins formed <b>before</b> or during deformation: early, refolded veins and syn-kinematic veins. They are not the gold veins. At Tuena, as across the Lachlan, the gold is in late, <b>post-kinematic</b>, flat, carbonate-rich veins that cut the foliation. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 39 and 40</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 13 Oct</span></p>", "e": "These veins formed before or during deformation: early, refolded veins and syn-kinematic veins. They are not the gold veins. At Tuena, as across the Lachlan, the gold is in late, post-kinematic, flat, carbonate-rich veins that cut the foliation."},
 {"id": "L13-obs-tuenagold", "kind": "obs", "lec": 13, "sec": "tuena", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L13/tuena-gold.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Reflected light view of grey matrix with thin dark fractures, along which small bright yellow grains sit, 200 micrometre scale bar"}], "img": "img/L13/tuena-gold.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 13 field task · Tuena under the microscope", "q": "What are the bright yellow grains, and where are they?", "seen": "<ul><li>Bright yellow, high-reflectance grains, irregular and long.</li><li>In lines along thin fractures, plus a few separate specks.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">This is <b>gold</b> in very small veinlets and disseminated grains, filling late fractures. The gold came in after the rock was already deformed and fractured, as the vein history shows. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 40</span></p>", "e": "This is gold in very small veinlets and disseminated grains, filling late fractures. The gold came in after the rock was already deformed and fractured, as the vein history shows."},
 {"id": "L14-obs-banded", "kind": "obs", "lec": 14, "sec": "what", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L14/banded.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Cut slab of a vein made of many thin, parallel bands of white, grey and pale blue quartz and chalcedony"}], "img": "img/L14/banded.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 14 field task · a vein specimen", "q": "Describe the banding. What does it tell you about how this vein filled?", "seen": "<ul><li>Many thin, roughly parallel bands of white quartz, and grey and clear blue-grey chalcedony, repeated across the slab.</li><li>No wall-rock fragments, and no single coarse filling.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">The vein filled a little at a time, band after band. Each layer grew on the one before, in open space. This is crustiform (colloform) banding. It is crack-seal behaviour: the vein opens, minerals are deposited, and it opens again. Each time, the fluid chemistry has changed a little, and this makes the very clear banding. Each band records a pulse of fluid, and fine chalcedony means fast deposition at low temperature. This is the typical texture of low sulphidation epithermal veins, and the gold is often in certain bands. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 1, 27 to 29</span></p>", "e": "The vein filled a little at a time, band after band. Each layer grew on the one before, in open space. This is crustiform (colloform) banding. It is crack-seal behaviour: the vein opens, minerals are deposited, and it opens again. Each time, the fluid chemistry has changed a little, and this makes the very clear banding. Each band records a pulse of fluid, and fine chalcedony means fast deposition at low temperature. This is the typical texture of low sulphidation epithermal veins, and the gold is often in certain bands."},
 {"id": "L14-obs-champagne", "kind": "obs", "lec": 14, "sec": "surface", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L14/champagne.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A steaming pale green-blue pool with a bright orange rim of precipitate"}], "img": "img/L14/champagne.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 14 field task · Champagne Pool", "q": "The orange edge of this pool at Waiotapu contains about 80 ppm gold. Why is nobody mining it?", "seen": "<ul><li>A steaming pool with a bright orange edge: sulphides of arsenic, antimony and mercury deposited from the water, containing gold and silver.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">80 ppm (80 g/t) is a high grade, but there is no <b>volume</b> to mine: only a thin crust on one pool. If you look closely, you can see the water boiling. This is a living epithermal system depositing gold right now. It is the surface end of something that could become an ore deposit if it is buried and preserved. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 7</span></p>", "e": "80 ppm (80 g/t) is a high grade, but there is no volume to mine: only a thin crust on one pool. If you look closely, you can see the water boiling. This is a living epithermal system depositing gold right now. It is the surface end of something that could become an ore deposit if it is buried and preserved."},
 {"id": "L14-obs-vughy", "kind": "obs", "lec": 14, "sec": "hs", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L14/vughy1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale brownish rock full of irregular holes and cavities, a pen for scale", "cap": "A · Veladero, Argentina"}, {"src": "img/L14/vughy2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale grey rock riddled with small angular holes where crystals used to be, a pen for scale", "cap": "B · El Indio district, Chile"}], "img": "img/L14/vughy1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 14 field task · a strange porous rock", "q": "The holes in B have the shape of crystals. What was in them? What removed it? What is left?", "seen": "<ul><li>Pale, light, porous rock. The holes in B are blocky and rectangular, with the shape of feldspar phenocrysts in a porphyry.</li><li>A: holes through a fine-grained breccia matrix and fragments.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">This is <b>vughy silica</b>. Extremely acidic fluid (pH below 2) dissolved the feldspar phenocrysts and everything else except silica. This left a skeleton of silica with holes in the shape of crystals. It is the core of high sulphidation alteration. Because it is porous, it is a very good trap for later fluid with gold and enargite. A is a diatreme breccia at Veladero, and B is a porphyry altered to vughy silica in the El Indio district. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 22</span></p>", "e": "This is vughy silica. Extremely acidic fluid (pH below 2) dissolved the feldspar phenocrysts and everything else except silica. This left a skeleton of silica with holes in the shape of crystals. It is the core of high sulphidation alteration. Because it is porous, it is a very good trap for later fluid with gold and enargite. A is a diatreme breccia at Veladero, and B is a porphyry altered to vughy silica in the El Indio district."},
 {"id": "L14-obs-bonanza", "kind": "obs", "lec": 14, "sec": "ls", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L14/ediecreek.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Fingers holding a piece of grey quartz vein with a central band of yellow gold filling the vein centre", "cap": "A · Edie Creek, PNG"}, {"src": "img/L14/porgera.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Grey rock with a mass of fine, wiry, branching gold and pale quartz, a pen for scale", "cap": "B · Porgera Zone VII, PNG"}], "img": "img/L14/ediecreek.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 14 field task · bonanza", "q": "Exactly where is the gold in A? What texture does the gold have in B?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: gold fills the centre line of an open quartz vein, which was the last space to close.</li><li>B: fine, branching wire gold with quartz and a green vanadium mica, roscoelite.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">Both are <b>bonanza</b> ore. In A, the gold was deposited last, in the open centre of the vein. In B, it grew as free wires into open space. Open space together with a sudden change in the fluid (boiling or mixing) gives these very high grades. Roscoelite is a good sign of ore in epithermal systems, as fuchsite is in orogenic gold. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 26</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 16 Oct</span></p>", "e": "Both are bonanza ore. In A, the gold was deposited last, in the open centre of the vein. In B, it grew as free wires into open space. Open space together with a sudden change in the fluid (boiling or mixing) gives these very high grades. Roscoelite is a good sign of ore in epithermal systems, as fuchsite is in orogenic gold."},
 {"id": "L14-obs-hishikari", "kind": "obs", "lec": 14, "sec": "ls", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L14/hishikari.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Two geologists in hard hats beside a wall of contorted white and grey banded quartz with darker fragments"}], "img": "img/L14/hishikari.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 14 field task · Hishikari", "q": "Look at the edges of the vein. As well as the banding, what else is in the vein?", "seen": "<ul><li>Thick, banded, white and grey quartz-adularia, folded in some places.</li><li>Angular dark fragments of wall rock inside the vein, near its edges.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">This is a banded adularia-sericite fissure vein with <b>floating clast breccias</b>. These are wall-rock fragments that broke off and were trapped as the vein grew. They are a sign of repeated fracturing and pulses of fluid. It is really a vein breccia: the vein itself has been brecciated. Hishikari in Japan, which was first mined for quartz, is one of the richest gold mines in the world and the classic example of low sulphidation. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 28 and 29</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 16 Oct</span></p>", "e": "This is a banded adularia-sericite fissure vein with floating clast breccias. These are wall-rock fragments that broke off and were trapped as the vein grew. They are a sign of repeated fracturing and pulses of fluid. It is really a vein breccia: the vein itself has been brecciated. Hishikari in Japan, which was first mined for quartz, is one of the richest gold mines in the world and the classic example of low sulphidation."},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-0", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/cass-twin.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Very heavy, hard, adamantine lustre, black, twinned at an angle. Name it.", "o": ["Wolframite", "Cassiterite", "Schorl", "Magnetite"], "a": 1, "e": "Cassiterite, with a classic knee twin. (Elsmore, NSW, slide 6)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-1", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/cass-needle.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Thin prismatic crystals of a very dense tin mineral. Which variety?", "o": ["Wood tin", "Ruby tin", "Needle tin", "Stannite"], "a": 2, "e": "Needle tin: prismatic cassiterite, Bolivia. (slide 6)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-2", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/stannite.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Massive, steel-grey metallic tin mineral from a vein rich in sulphide. Name it.", "o": ["Galena", "Stannite", "Molybdenite", "Bismuthinite"], "a": 1, "e": "Stannite, Cu₂FeSnS₄. (Cornwall, slide 10)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-3", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/sch-xl.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Golden orange, vitreous to adamantine lustre, heavy for its colour, shaped like an octahedron. Name it.", "o": ["Fluorite", "Scheelite", "Topaz", "Calcite"], "a": 1, "e": "Scheelite, CaWO₄. Check it with a UV lamp. (slide 14)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-4", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/ferberite.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Black, blade-shaped, with lines (striations), very heavy, on quartz. Name it.", "o": ["Ferberite (wolframite)", "Schorl", "Cassiterite", "Hematite"], "a": 0, "e": "Ferberite, the Fe end of wolframite. Schorl is lighter, with triangular cross-sections. (slide 16)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-5", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/hubnerite.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Red-brown, blade-shaped crystals of a heavy tungsten mineral. Name it.", "o": ["Hübnerite", "Ruby tin", "Rutile", "Cinnabar"], "a": 0, "e": "Hübnerite, MnWO₄. (slide 16)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-6", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/bismuthinite.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Soft, lead-grey, radiating metallic needles. Name it.", "o": ["Stibnite", "Bismuthinite", "Molybdenite", "Galena"], "a": 1, "e": "Bismuthinite, Bi₂S₃. Stibnite looks very similar. The setting (a granite Sn-W system) helps. (slide 20)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-7", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/bismuth.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Very dense, soft, silvery with a pinkish colour and strong cleavage. Name it.", "o": ["Native bismuth", "Native silver", "Galena", "Arsenopyrite"], "a": 0, "e": "Native bismuth: reddish-white tint, SG ~9.8. (slide 20)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-8", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/molybdenite.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Extremely soft, flexible, greasy grey hexagonal plates. Name it.", "o": ["Graphite", "Molybdenite", "Biotite", "Hematite"], "a": 1, "e": "Molybdenite: denser and more metallic than graphite. (Kingsgate, NSW, slide 23)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-9", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/ferrimolybdite.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Bright yellow powder on weathered Mo ore. Name it.", "o": ["Sulphur", "Ferrimolybdite", "Jarosite", "Scheelite"], "a": 1, "e": "Ferrimolybdite, which forms when molybdenite is altered. (slide 23)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-10", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/ilsemannite.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Pale blue powdery coating on oxidised molybdenite ore. Name it.", "o": ["Azurite", "Chrysocolla", "Ilsemannite", "Fluorite"], "a": 2, "e": "Ilsemannite, a blue mineral that forms when molybdenum minerals are altered. (Carinthia, Austria, slide 23)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-11", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/beryl.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Pale blue hexagonal prism with mica, from a granite system. Name it.", "o": ["Beryl (aquamarine)", "Apatite", "Topaz", "Quartz"], "a": 0, "e": "Beryl, var. aquamarine, with muscovite. (slide 27)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-12", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/tourmaline.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Black prism with lines along it and a rounded triangular cross-section. Name it.", "o": ["Ferberite", "Schorl tourmaline", "Hornblende", "Cassiterite"], "a": 1, "e": "Schorl: lines along its length, a triangular cross-section, and lighter than wolframite. (slide 27)"},
 {"id": "L15-obs-woodtin", "kind": "obs", "lec": 15, "sec": "tin", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L15/cass-wood.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A slab with concentric bands of cream and yellow-brown to dark brown material in a wood-grain pattern"}], "img": "img/L15/cass-wood.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 15 field task · wood tin", "q": "This does not look like the tin ore you would expect. Describe the banding, and suggest how it formed.", "seen": "<ul><li>Round, wavy bands that look like wood grain, cream and yellow-brown to dark brown, with a fibrous texture.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">This is <b>wood tin</b>, a fibrous, colloform cassiterite from Cornwall. The bands record minerals deposited one after another from a hydrothermal vein: quartz, then cassiterite, then quartz, then cassiterite, again and again. It looks nothing like normal cassiterite, and this is why you should remember it. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 7</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 26 Oct</span></p>", "e": "This is wood tin, a fibrous, colloform cassiterite from Cornwall. The bands record minerals deposited one after another from a hydrothermal vein: quartz, then cassiterite, then quartz, then cassiterite, again and again. It looks nothing like normal cassiterite, and this is why you should remember it."},
 {"id": "L15-obs-uv", "kind": "obs", "lec": 15, "sec": "tungsten", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L15/sch-massive.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale buff, granular rock with yellow-brown patches", "cap": "A · daylight"}, {"src": "img/L15/sch-uv.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Bright blue-white glowing patches on a black background", "cap": "B · under UV light"}], "img": "img/L15/sch-massive.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 15 field task · night prospecting", "q": "In daylight, A is easy to miss. What does B show? Why is this useful in the field?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: a plain yellow-brown granular mass.</li><li>B: the same kind of material glowing bright blue-white.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">It is <b>scheelite</b>, which glows (fluoresces) blue-white under short-wave UV light. At night, a UV lamp makes scheelite in outcrop, core or a waste dump light up against the minerals around it. This is a cheap and fast way to find and map tungsten. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 14</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 26 Oct</span></p>", "e": "It is scheelite, which glows (fluoresces) blue-white under short-wave UV light. At night, a UV lamp makes scheelite in outcrop, core or a waste dump light up against the minerals around it. This is a cheap and fast way to find and map tungsten."},
 {"id": "L15-obs-topaz", "kind": "obs", "lec": 15, "sec": "gangue", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L15/topaz.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A clear, colourless crystal with a rough, pitted and frosted surface instead of sharp faces"}], "img": "img/L15/topaz.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 15 field task · a damaged crystal", "q": "This colourless topaz has lost its sharp faces. What happened to it?", "seen": "<ul><li>Clear, colourless topaz with rounded, frosted surfaces with small holes.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">It has been <b>etched</b>: partly dissolved by a later hydrothermal fluid with a very low pH. These systems do not have only one fluid. They have several pulses of fluid, and later pulses can attack the minerals that earlier pulses grew. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 27</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 26 Oct</span></p>", "e": "It has been etched: partly dissolved by a later hydrothermal fluid with a very low pH. These systems do not have only one fluid. They have several pulses of fluid, and later pulses can attack the minerals that earlier pulses grew."},
 {"id": "L15-obs-greisen", "kind": "obs", "lec": 15, "sec": "greisen", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L15/greisen.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale cream to yellowish, granular rock with no pink or white feldspar visible"}], "img": "img/L15/greisen.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 15 field task · a greisen", "q": "This rock was once granite. Which minerals are missing, and what replaced them?", "seen": "<ul><li>A pale, granular rock, yellowish in some places, with no visible pink K-feldspar or white plagioclase.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">The feldspars have gone, and <b>quartz and muscovite</b> have replaced them. The yellowish colour is the muscovite. This is a greisen, and tin and tungsten minerals are usually found in greisens. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 31</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 26 Oct</span></p>", "e": "The feldspars have gone, and quartz and muscovite have replaced them. The yellowish colour is the muscovite. This is a greisen, and tin and tungsten minerals are usually found in greisens."},
 {"id": "L15-obs-tallebung", "kind": "obs", "lec": 15, "sec": "tallebung", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L15/tb-cass.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Close-up of a white quartz vein with black patches and grains, a scale card beside it"}], "img": "img/L15/tb-cass.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 15 field task · a Tallebung vein", "q": "What are the black patches in this quartz? Why would processing this ore be simple?", "seen": "<ul><li>Milky white quartz with coarse, black, shiny patches and grains.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">The black mineral is <b>cassiterite</b> (with ferberite). Both are coarse and very dense. So you crush the quartz and separate them by gravity, almost like panning. No complicated chemistry is needed. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 42</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 26 Oct</span></p>", "e": "The black mineral is cassiterite (with ferberite). Both are coarse and very dense. So you crush the quartz and separate them by gravity, almost like panning. No complicated chemistry is needed."},
 {"id": "L16-obs-colloform", "kind": "obs", "lec": 16, "sec": "families", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L16/colloform.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Cut slab with concentric cream, yellow and brown bands wrapped around dark grey patches, 2 cm scale bar"}], "img": "img/L16/colloform.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 16 field task · banded zinc ore", "q": "Describe the banding. Did these minerals grow into open space, or did they replace something solid?", "seen": "<ul><li>Round, curved, cream to yellow-brown bands around darker grey patches, like the layers of an onion.</li><li>Branching dark grey growths between some of the bands.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">The pale bands are colloform sphalerite (pale, with little iron), and the branching grey growths are dendritic galena. Both grew into <b>open space</b>, layer by layer. This is MVT ore from Upper Silesia, Poland. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 28</span> (Leach et al., 2005)</p>", "e": "The pale bands are colloform sphalerite (pale, with little iron), and the branching grey growths are dendritic galena. Both grew into open space, layer by layer. This is MVT ore from Upper Silesia, Poland. (Leach et al., 2005)"},
 {"id": "L16-obs-cmpvcore", "kind": "obs", "lec": 16, "sec": "cmpvmin", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L16/cmpv-core.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Six panels: A, a drill core of pale bleached rock with grey and white veins and dark green patches; B to F, microscope images"}], "img": "img/L16/cmpv-core.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 16 field task · a CMPV core", "q": "Look at A. Describe the colour of the host rock and what crosses it. What would you record in your log?", "seen": "<ul><li>Pale, bleached siltstone (the Fe has been removed).</li><li>A quartz vein with siderite and disseminated sulphide, a thin massive sulphide veinlet, and dark green chlorite.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">You would record a bleached siltstone cut by a quartz-siderite vein with disseminated sulphide and a massive sulphide veinlet, plus chlorite. This is typical CMPV gangue and ore, and the bleaching helps you find the ore over a larger area. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 13</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 27 Oct</span></p>", "e": "You would record a bleached siltstone cut by a quartz-siderite vein with disseminated sulphide and a massive sulphide veinlet, plus chlorite. This is typical CMPV gangue and ore, and the bleaching helps you find the ore over a larger area."},
 {"id": "L16-obs-webbsrl", "kind": "obs", "lec": 16, "sec": "cmpvmin", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L16/webbs-rl.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Reflected light: a broad bright white grain on the right with black triangular pits, irregular pale grey grains on the left with a small yellow patch, all in dark grey gangue, 200 micrometre scale bar"}], "img": "img/L16/webbs-rl.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 16 field task · Webbs ore in reflected light", "q": "Find the black triangles in the bright grain on the right. Which mineral do they identify? What is the small yellow patch on the left?", "seen": "<ul><li>A bright white grain with black triangular pits in rows.</li><li>Irregular pale grey grains on the left with a small brassy yellow patch, in dark grey gangue that does not reflect light.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">The bright grain is <b>galena</b>: the triangular pits, made when polishing pulled out pieces along its cubic cleavage, identify it. The yellow patch is <b>chalcopyrite</b>, and the dark background is quartz-carbonate gangue. Freibergite, arsenopyrite, and sphalerite with stannite are the other minerals labelled from Webbs on this slide. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 12</span></p>", "e": "The bright grain is galena: the triangular pits, made when polishing pulled out pieces along its cubic cleavage, identify it. The yellow patch is chalcopyrite, and the dark background is quartz-carbonate gangue. Freibergite, arsenopyrite, and sphalerite with stannite are the other minerals labelled from Webbs on this slide."},
 {"id": "L16-obs-breccia", "kind": "obs", "lec": 16, "sec": "mvtore", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L16/mvt-breccia.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A cut slab of angular pale carbonate blocks separated by thin dark sulphide-filled gaps, a coin for scale"}], "img": "img/L16/mvt-breccia.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 16 field task · a breccia", "q": "The blocks are carbonate, and the dark seams are sulphide. What made the breccia, and when did the sulphide arrive?", "seen": "<ul><li>Angular blocks of pale carbonate that fit back together, with thin dark sulphide seams between them.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">This is a <b>dissolution collapse breccia</b>. The carbonate was dissolved, and the roof collapsed into blocks. Then hydrothermal fluid altered the breccia and filled and replaced the gaps with sulphide. The collapse came first, and the ore came second. Upper Silesia, Poland. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 28</span> (Leach et al., 2005)</p>", "e": "This is a dissolution collapse breccia. The carbonate was dissolved, and the roof collapsed into blocks. Then hydrothermal fluid altered the breccia and filled and replaced the gaps with sulphide. The collapse came first, and the ore came second. Upper Silesia, Poland. (Leach et al., 2005)"},
 {"id": "L16-obs-manukapit", "kind": "obs", "lec": 16, "sec": "manuka", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L16/manuka-pit.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Composite of open-pit photos labelled saprolite, oxide zone, pyritic shale, dolomitic limestone, Gundaroo sandstone and sulfides"}], "img": "img/L16/manuka-pit.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 16 field task · the pit wall", "q": "From top to bottom, what layers can you see in the pit? Which one contains the silver that is mined?", "seen": "<ul><li>Weathered saprolite and an oxide zone at the top, then black pyritic shale, then dolomitic limestone, above sandstone.</li><li>Pockets of sulphide in the limestone.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">The silver that is mined is in the <b>clays of the oxide zone</b> above the limestone. Below are the two primary styles: pyrite-rich black shale, and galena-sphalerite in the dolomitic limestone. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 49</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 27 Oct</span></p>", "e": "The silver that is mined is in the clays of the oxide zone above the limestone. Below are the two primary styles: pyrite-rich black shale, and galena-sphalerite in the dolomitic limestone."},
 {"id": "L16-obs-manukarl", "kind": "obs", "lec": 16, "sec": "manuka", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L16/framboids.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Electron image of clusters of tiny spheres made of even tinier crystals, 10 micrometre scale", "cap": "A · SEM, scale 10 µm"}, {"src": "img/L16/covellite.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Reflected light view with blue patches in a dull olive matrix and a bright yellow speck", "cap": "B · reflected light, scale 50 µm"}], "img": "img/L16/framboids.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 16 field task · under the microscope", "q": "What are the raspberry-shaped spheres in A? What is the blue mineral in B?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: spheres a few to tens of µm across, each full of tiny crystals.</li><li>B: patches with a strong indigo-blue colour in reflected light.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">A is <b>framboidal pyrite</b>. It is typical of sediment that formed at low temperature, rich in organic matter and affected by bacteria, like Manuka's black shale. The blue in B is <b>covellite</b> (CuS), which is easy to recognise in reflected light. Chalcopyrite is rare here. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 51 and 52</span></p>", "e": "A is framboidal pyrite. It is typical of sediment that formed at low temperature, rich in organic matter and affected by bacteria, like Manuka's black shale. The blue in B is covellite (CuS), which is easy to recognise in reflected light. Chalcopyrite is rare here."},
 {"id": "log-mkCards-0", "kind": "core", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s036_1.jpg", "lbl": "End of a tray", "q": "What is written here?", "o": ["Tray identification", "Drillers’ depth block", "Metre mark-ups", "SG test mark", "Core loss", "Orientation line"], "a": 0, "e": "Hole name and tray number, so the tray can always be found and laid out in order. (slide 36)"},
 {"id": "log-mkCards-1", "kind": "core", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s037_1.jpg", "lbl": "Between two pieces of core", "q": "Who put this block here, and why?", "o": ["Tray identification", "Drillers’ depth block", "Metre mark-ups", "SG test mark", "Core loss", "Orientation line"], "a": 1, "e": "The drillers mark the depth at the end of each run. (slide 37)"},
 {"id": "log-mkCards-2", "kind": "core", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s038_1.jpg", "lbl": "On the side of the tray", "q": "What are the blue ticks?", "o": ["Tray identification", "Drillers’ depth block", "Metre mark-ups", "SG test mark", "Core loss", "Orientation line"], "a": 2, "e": "Geologist’s metre mark-ups. Check them: mistakes are common. (slides 38 and 51)"},
 {"id": "log-mkCards-3", "kind": "core", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s039_1.jpg", "lbl": "Painted on the core", "q": "What do the white letters and lines show?", "o": ["Tray identification", "Drillers’ depth block", "Metre mark-ups", "SG test mark", "Core loss", "Orientation line"], "a": 3, "e": "Where a piece was taken for specific gravity (density) testing. (slide 39)"},
 {"id": "log-mkCards-4", "kind": "core", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s040_1.jpg", "lbl": "A red block in the tray", "q": "What does this block record?", "o": ["Tray identification", "Drillers’ depth block", "Metre mark-ups", "SG test mark", "Core loss", "Orientation line"], "a": 4, "e": "10 cm of hole with no core recovered. (slide 40)"},
 {"id": "log-mkCards-5", "kind": "core", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s043_1.jpg", "lbl": "Drawn along the core", "q": "What is the long line?", "o": ["Tray identification", "Drillers’ depth block", "Metre mark-ups", "SG test mark", "Core loss", "Orientation line"], "a": 5, "e": "The drillers’ orientation line, arrows pointing down hole. (slide 43)"},
 {"id": "log-obs-rabpiles", "kind": "obs", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "methods", "imgs": [{"src": "img/logging/s007_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A drill rig in scrub behind long rows of small piles of crushed rock on red ground, changing colour from red to orange to pale cream along each row"}], "img": "img/logging/s007_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Drill core logging field task · rows of piles", "q": "What is each little pile, and what does the colour change along a row tell you? How much would you trust these samples?", "seen": "<ul><li>Long rows of small piles of rock chips laid out in front of the rig.</li><li>Along each row the colour changes from red-brown to orange to pale cream.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So each pile is the cuttings from one metre of a RAB hole, and each row is one hole read from the top down: the colour change is you passing down through the weathering profile. Useful for a first look, but RAB samples are low quality. They come up outside the rods, so chips from higher in the hole can mix in. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 7, 9 and 27</span></p>", "e": "So each pile is the cuttings from one metre of a RAB hole, and each row is one hole read from the top down: the colour change is you passing down through the weathering profile. Useful for a first look, but RAB samples are low quality. They come up outside the rods, so chips from higher in the hole can mix in."},
 {"id": "log-obs-oriline", "kind": "obs", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "orient", "imgs": [{"src": "img/logging/s043_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale grey core in a tray with a long straight red line along it and short angled red strokes off the line"}], "img": "img/logging/s043_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Drill core logging field task · red lines", "q": "What is the long red line, and what are the short angled strokes for?", "seen": "<ul><li>A straight red line along the length of the core.</li><li>Short red strokes angled off it, like arrowheads, all pointing the same way.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s the orientation line, and the strokes are arrows pointing <b>down the hole</b>. Every structure you measure on this core is measured from that line, so you always know which way was down and which side was the bottom. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 41 to 43</span></p>", "e": "So it’s the orientation line, and the strokes are arrows pointing down the hole. Every structure you measure on this core is measured from that line, so you always know which way was down and which side was the bottom."},
 {"id": "log-obs-weathered", "kind": "obs", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "intervals", "imgs": [{"src": "img/logging/s055_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale orange-pink, finely cracked core beside a centimetre scale", "cap": "A · 12 m"}, {"src": "img/logging/s056_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Broken blocks of yellow-brown and grey rock with a crusty surface beside a scale", "cap": "B · 23.0 m"}], "img": "img/logging/s055_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Drill core logging field task · the top of the hole", "q": "Both are from the weathered zone. What has happened to A, and what does the slide call the depth in B?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: soft-looking, pale pink to orange, cracked all over, no fresh minerals visible.</li><li>B: broken, crumbly blocks with yellow-brown iron staining and some grey rock.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So A is completely weathered rock: <b>kaolinite and hematite</b> from weathering, the clay-and-iron-oxide top of the profile. B marks the <b>base of oxidation</b> at 23 m: below here the sulphides are fresh. Log it carefully, because the oxide and primary ores need different processing. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 55 and 56</span></p>", "e": "So A is completely weathered rock: kaolinite and hematite from weathering, the clay-and-iron-oxide top of the profile. B marks the base of oxidation at 23 m: below here the sulphides are fresh. Log it carefully, because the oxide and primary ores need different processing."},
 {"id": "log-obs-faults", "kind": "obs", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "intervals", "imgs": [{"src": "img/logging/s057_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Core with a band of crushed, granular grey material running between two blocks, beside a scale card", "cap": "A · 40.4 to 40.6 m"}, {"src": "img/logging/s058_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A tray of several rows of core where one long row is broken into rubble and small fragments", "cap": "B · a whole row of broken core"}], "img": "img/logging/s057_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Drill core logging field task · two broken zones", "q": "A is a 20 cm crushed zone. B is broken over a long length of core. What are they, and why is B so long?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: a narrow band of crushed, granular material, finer than the rock either side.</li><li>B: rubble and fragments along most of a tray row, with intact core above and below.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So A is a <b>shear zone with gouge</b>, the ground-up rock along a fault. B is a fault zone running <b>sub-parallel to the core axis</b>: the hole went down along the fault instead of across it, so you get metres of broken core and almost no information about the fault’s orientation. That’s why you angle holes across the expected structures. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 30, 57 and 58</span></p>", "e": "So A is a shear zone with gouge, the ground-up rock along a fault. B is a fault zone running sub-parallel to the core axis: the hole went down along the fault instead of across it, so you get metres of broken core and almost no information about the fault’s orientation. That’s why you angle holes across the expected structures."},
 {"id": "log-obs-folded", "kind": "obs", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "intervals", "imgs": [{"src": "img/logging/s059_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark grey core with white veins twisting into tight loops and hooks, beside a scale"}], "img": "img/logging/s059_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Drill core logging field task · 221 m", "q": "Log the host rock and the veins. What happened to the veins after they formed?", "seen": "<ul><li>Dark grey, fine-grained host rock.</li><li>White veins bent into tight loops and hooks.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So these are <b>folded quartz veins in siltstone</b>. The veins formed first, then the rock was deformed and they folded with it, so the veining is older than (or early in) the deformation. That relative timing matters for orogenic gold, where you want to know which vein generation carries the gold. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 59</span></p>", "e": "So these are folded quartz veins in siltstone. The veins formed first, then the rock was deformed and they folded with it, so the veining is older than (or early in) the deformation. That relative timing matters for orogenic gold, where you want to know which vein generation carries the gold."},
 {"id": "log-obs-gypsum", "kind": "obs", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "intervals", "imgs": [{"src": "img/logging/s060_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pinkish-grey core crossed by a thin pale curved vein, with a red line along the top of the core, beside a scale"}], "img": "img/logging/s060_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Drill core logging field task · 802.6 m", "q": "The thin pale vein is gypsum. Estimate its alpha angle. Is it a good structure to measure?", "seen": "<ul><li>A thin pale vein crossing the core almost straight across.</li><li>A red line along the core: the orientation (or cut) line.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So the vein is at about <b>80° to the core axis</b>, nearly perpendicular. It cuts right through the core, so it’s measurable: alpha 80°, and with the orientation line you could get beta too. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 47 and 60</span></p>", "e": "So the vein is at about 80° to the core axis, nearly perpendicular. It cuts right through the core, so it’s measurable: alpha 80°, and with the orientation line you could get beta too."},
 {"id": "log-obs-vg", "kind": "obs", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "intervals", "imgs": [{"src": "img/logging/s061_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Fine-grained grey metallic core with several small blue pen circles drawn on it"}], "img": "img/logging/s061_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Drill core logging field task · 375.9 m", "q": "The core is massive sulphide. What do you think the blue circles mark, and why bother drawing them?", "seen": "<ul><li>Fine-grained, grey, metallic rock with no gangue to speak of.</li><li>Small blue circles drawn around tiny bright specks.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s <b>massive sulphide with visible gold</b>, each grain circled in blue. Visible gold is logged and marked so nobody misses it and the interval is sampled carefully. Because of the nugget effect, gold core is often assayed whole rather than halved. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 17 and 61</span></p>", "e": "So it’s massive sulphide with visible gold, each grain circled in blue. Visible gold is logged and marked so nobody misses it and the interval is sampled carefully. Because of the nugget effect, gold core is often assayed whole rather than halved."},
 {"id": "log-obs-bowdens", "kind": "obs", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "storage", "imgs": [{"src": "img/logging/s063_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Core broken into chunks with brown and orange staining and dark patches", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/logging/s063_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Fresh greenish-grey core with pale angular fragments in a fine matrix", "cap": "B"}], "img": "img/logging/s063_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Drill core logging field task · same deposit, different years", "q": "Both are tuff-breccia from the Bowdens silver deposit. One was drilled in 2021, the other in 2025. Which is which, and what happened to the older one?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: brown and orange stained, broken, the original texture hard to see.</li><li>B: fresh, greenish grey, with clear pale fragments in a fine matrix.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So A is the older core, drilled in <b>2021</b> (164.7 m), and B the <b>2025</b> core (102.5 m). A’s sulphides have oxidised in storage, staining it and breaking it up; a few years in a core yard was enough. Log and photograph core as soon as it comes out. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 62 and 63</span></p>", "e": "So A is the older core, drilled in 2021 (164.7 m), and B the 2025 core (102.5 m). A’s sulphides have oxidised in storage, staining it and breaking it up; a few years in a core yard was enough. Log and photograph core as soon as it comes out."},
 {"id": "log-obs-decay", "kind": "obs", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "storage", "imgs": [{"src": "img/logging/s065_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A tray of pale cream core crazed with cracks like dried mud", "cap": "A · slide 65"}, {"src": "img/logging/s067_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Core with patches of bright sky-blue crust on pale and red-brown rock, beside a scale", "cap": "B · slide 67"}], "img": "img/logging/s065_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Drill core logging field task · two more casualties", "q": "What has gone wrong in each tray?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: pale core crazed with cracks all over, falling apart.</li><li>B: bright blue crusts growing on the core.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So A has been destroyed by abundant fine-grained, disseminated <b>pyrite and marcasite</b> oxidising (marcasite is especially unstable). B is encrusted with <b>copper sulphates</b> that grew after drilling as copper sulphides oxidised. Neither tells you anything about the original ore unless someone logged it first. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 65 to 67</span></p>", "e": "So A has been destroyed by abundant fine-grained, disseminated pyrite and marcasite oxidising (marcasite is especially unstable). B is encrusted with copper sulphates that grew after drilling as copper sulphides oxidised. Neither tells you anything about the original ore unless someone logged it first."},
 {"id": "alt-stCards-0", "kind": "core", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s057_1.jpg", "lbl": "Type", "q": "What type of structure is the crushed band?", "o": ["Bedding", "Shear zone with gouge", "Cross-bedding"], "a": 1, "e": "Ground-up rock along a fault or shear. (logging deck, slide 57)"},
 {"id": "alt-stCards-1", "kind": "core", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s059_1.jpg", "lbl": "Primary or secondary?", "q": "Are the folded veins primary or secondary structures?", "o": ["Primary", "Secondary"], "a": 1, "e": "Veins and folds form after the rock does, so both are secondary. (logging deck, slide 59)"},
 {"id": "alt-stCards-2", "kind": "core", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s060_1.jpg", "lbl": "Spacing", "q": "How would you describe the spacing of veining in this piece?", "o": ["Closely spaced fracture zone", "A single, isolated vein", "Pervasive"], "a": 1, "e": "One thin gypsum vein: note its angle (about 80° to the core axis). (logging deck, slide 60)"},
 {"id": "alt-stCards-3", "kind": "core", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s058_1.jpg", "lbl": "Mining impact", "q": "A broad fault zone of broken core like this matters to the mine because...", "o": ["It is a zone of weakness that can cause ground failure", "It makes the ore richer", "It has no effect"], "a": 0, "e": "Faults and fracture zones are engineering hazards. (lab notes)"},
 {"id": "alt-obs-dominion", "kind": "obs", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "oxid", "imgs": [{"src": "img/alteration/gossan.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A geologist in a hard hat crouching in a shallow pit dug into red-brown soil and rubble in scrubland, with sample bags beside the pit"}], "img": "img/alteration/gossan.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Alteration lab field task · a gold gossan", "q": "This pit is dug into an exposed gossan in the southern Cobar Basin. What colour is the material, and which iron oxide would you expect to dominate it?", "seen": "<ul><li>Red-brown to orange-brown soil and rubble, much redder than the scrubland around it.</li><li>Sample bags lined up beside the pit.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So this is the Dominion Prospect gold gossan, and the red-brown colour points to <b>hematite</b> and goethite from oxidised sulphides. Reddish hematite suggests moving water. The bags are there because gossans are sampled for the metals that stay behind, gold among them. <span class=\"srcs\">lab notes, oxidation</span></p>", "e": "So this is the Dominion Prospect gold gossan, and the red-brown colour points to hematite and goethite from oxidised sulphides. Reddish hematite suggests moving water. The bags are there because gossans are sampled for the metals that stay behind, gold among them."},
 {"id": "alt-obs-busai", "kind": "obs", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "types", "imgs": [{"src": "img/alteration/propylitic.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A grey-green outcrop surface with yellow-green patches, a geological hammer lying across it"}], "img": "img/alteration/propylitic.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Alteration lab field task · Busai, Woodlark Island", "q": "Name the two colours in this rock and the minerals that make them.", "seen": "<ul><li>Patchy yellow-green and duller grey to olive green over the whole surface.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s <b>propylitic</b> alteration: yellow-green <b>epidote</b> and grey-green <b>chlorite</b>. <span class=\"srcs\">lab notes, propylitic</span></p>", "e": "So it’s propylitic alteration: yellow-green epidote and grey-green chlorite."},
 {"id": "alt-obs-hera", "kind": "obs", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "types", "imgs": [{"src": "img/alteration/phyllic.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground face with a pale grey to white layer above dark rock, a scale card at the bottom"}], "img": "img/alteration/phyllic.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Alteration lab field task · Hera mine", "q": "The pale layer was once a dark siltstone. What gives it its colour and sheen?", "seen": "<ul><li>A pale, almost white band with a soft sheen, sharply above darker rock.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s <b>phyllic</b>-altered siltstone, made of fine illite-muscovite and quartz. Phyllosilicates crystallising in the rock give it that sheen. <span class=\"srcs\">lab notes, phyllic</span></p>", "e": "So it’s phyllic -altered siltstone, made of fine illite-muscovite and quartz. Phyllosilicates crystallising in the rock give it that sheen."},
 {"id": "alt-obs-drake", "kind": "obs", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "types", "imgs": [{"src": "img/alteration/potassic.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A section of drill core with a pale pink to salmon colour and fine grey speckles, beside a scale"}], "img": "img/alteration/potassic.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Alteration lab field task · Drake goldfield", "q": "What mineral makes this core pink?", "seen": "<ul><li>Pale pink to salmon core with fine grey patches.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s <b>potassic</b> alteration: the pink comes from hydrothermal <b>K-feldspar</b>, commonly the low-temperature variety adularia. <span class=\"srcs\">lab notes, potassic</span></p>", "e": "So it’s potassic alteration: the pink comes from hydrothermal K-feldspar, commonly the low-temperature variety adularia."},
 {"id": "alt-obs-bowdensarg", "kind": "obs", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "types", "imgs": [{"src": "img/alteration/argillic.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale grey, crumbly, soft-looking core with a rough surface, beside a scale"}], "img": "img/alteration/argillic.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Alteration lab field task · Bowdens silver deposit", "q": "The core is pale and crumbly. What happened to its feldspar?", "seen": "<ul><li>Very pale grey, rough and crumbly, weak-looking core.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s <b>argillic</b> alteration with abundant <b>illite</b>: the feldspar has broken down to clay, which bleaches and badly weakens the rock. <span class=\"srcs\">lab notes, argillic</span></p>", "e": "So it’s argillic alteration with abundant illite: the feldspar has broken down to clay, which bleaches and badly weakens the rock."},
 {"id": "alt-obs-bukit", "kind": "obs", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "types", "imgs": [{"src": "img/alteration/advarg.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A cut slab with pure white, yellow-white, red and yellow-brown patches, beside a ruler"}], "img": "img/alteration/advarg.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Alteration lab field task · Bukit Jambi, Sumatra", "q": "Four colours: pure white, yellow-white, red and yellow-brown. Match each to a mineral.", "seen": "<ul><li>Pure white, yellow-white, red and yellow-brown patches swirled together.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s <b>advanced argillic</b> alteration: <b>alunite</b> (yellow-white), <b>hematite</b> (red), <b>goethite</b> (yellow-brown) and <b>kaolinite</b> (pure white). <span class=\"srcs\">lab notes, advanced argillic</span></p>", "e": "So it’s advanced argillic alteration: alunite (yellow-white), hematite (red), goethite (yellow-brown) and kaolinite (pure white)."}
];
